{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014q1t3SJVaSd5sGpuMZAWuP","type":"tool_result","content":"data/anim:\nLICENSES\nSOURCES.md\nactions\ncatalogs\ncmu\nsets\nupgrade.json\n\ndata/anim/actions:\nbackflip.json\nbored.json\ncharge.json\ndeathBlown.json\ndeathCrush.json\ndeathPop.json\ndeathShock.json\ndeathShrink.json\ndeathSink.json\ndeathSpin.json\ndeathTopple.json\ndizzy.json\ngetUp.json\ngrab.json\ngrabbed.json\ngroundPound.json\nhurt.json\nitemGet.json\njump.json\nlandHeavy.json\nledgeHang.json\npant.json\nrise.json\nroll.json\nshiver.json\nspinJump.json\ntechRoll.json\nwallSlide.json\n\ndata/anim/sets:\ncmu\nmesh2motion\nquaternius\nstyle100\nstyle100_back\nstyle100_backrun\nstyle100_idle\nstyle100_run\nstyle100_side\nstyle100_siderun\n16: * `entities` and `get` → the world, read-only (a query names an undeclared component with the closest); `seed(n)`\n75:  seed: number;\n93:  seed(n?: number): number;\n178:    help: \"The engine's identity and health (version, runtime, adapter, features, downgrades, deps) and the run: scene, seed, tick, hz.\",\n188:      seed: h.session.world.seed,\n221:  seed: {\n222:    help: \"Starts the scene again with seed n (keeping the settings changed since it started) when n is given; returns the run's seed.\",\n224:      n: { type: 'integer', minimum: 0, description: 'The new seed: a whole number, 0 or more, as replays hold it.' },\n228:      return h.session.world.seed;\n/**\n * @file The engine without a page (PLAN.md §8.3, §6.9, WPs 1.6 and 2.7; doctrine: Verifiable): `createHeadless({\n * scene, seed, settings, view })` starts a scene as a recorded session (engine/sim/scene.ts) and returns its\n * inspector, the same object a page publishes as `window.__engine` minus a renderer: `render` and `shot` throw\n * `DEV_NO_RENDERER`. With `view: true` it also builds the scene's presentation graph in Node (engine/app/view.ts: the\n * stage and its camera, no GPU), so `scene.dump()` and `camera` work headless and view logic is tested in T1; the\n * scene's level must be registered first (`APP_NO_LEVEL`). `x sim` runs scenes through it, and tests drive the sim\n * with the same calls an agent makes in a page.\n *\n * Time: a headless engine has a frame clock over the run's settings, like a page, but no frames. `step(n, intents)`\n * runs n steps at once, inside the session, so the steps are recorded and `headlessHost(h).session.record()` is the run\n * as a replay. `pause`, `resume` and `timeScale` set the clock's settings as in a page (no real time passes headless,\n * so only `step` runs steps). After each step the run hands the hit-stop its rules asked for (engine/sim/damage.ts\n * `takeHitStop`) to its clock, whose budget shrinks stops that follow closely, so a page's frames stop and a crowd hit\n * stutters once; a page's real-time `?replay=` playback hands them over the same way (`stepped`), and a dev action's\n * goes with the next step, as in that playback. `seed(n)` starts the scene again with seed n, a new session and clock,\n * keeping every setting that differs from what a fresh start gives; `replay(text)` starts the replay's run (its\n * scene, seed and settings, keeping the view settings that differ, which never change play: a page's look stays) and\n * plays it, with a page's loop held, so it drops the stops it asks for (none is left to freeze a later frame). The\n * run's stats meter (engine/dev/stats.ts, `__engine.stats()`) times every step it runs, and the world's systems\n * through the world's probe; a page times its frames through it and gives it the renderer's counts (`Drawing.counts`).\n *\n * `createAppHost` is the run behind both engines: engine/app/engine.ts adds a page's drawing to it. Invariants:\n * everything runs synchronously after the promise resolves (the fdlibm swap holds only through synchronous entry\n * points); runs share nothing but the registry; the inspector holds only its members, so the run behind it is reached\n * through `headlessHost(h)` (tools only), whose `session` is what members get: the recorded operations and the world\n * read-only (engine/dev/members.ts). Its `restore` and `act` move entities between steps, so they snap the view to the\n * world (no interpolation from stale places), as a restart does. A seed is a whole number from 0 (`SIM_BAD_SEED`).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\n * import { defineScene } from '../sim/scene';\n * const demo = defineScene('demo', { setup: (w) => void w.spawn() }, createRegistry());\n * const h = await createHeadless({ scene: demo, seed: 3 });\n * h.step(60); // 60: the tick after the steps\n * h.entities(); // [{ id: 1, components: [] }]\n * @see engine/app/headless.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, log as sharedLog, type Log } from '../core/log';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, sameValue, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { isPlainObject } from '../core/schema';\nimport { createSettings, defineSettings, type SettingValue } from '../core/settings';\nimport { createClock, TIME_SETTINGS, type Clock } from '../core/time';\nimport '../dev/actions'; // registers `__engine.actions` and `__engine.bot`\nimport { createInspector, type Inspector } from '../dev/inspector';\nimport { recordedRun, type InspectorHost, type RecordedRun } from '../dev/members';\nimport { createStatsMeter, type RenderCounts, type StatsMeter } from '../dev/stats';\nimport { createMaterialLibrary, type MaterialLibrary } from '../gfx/materials/library';\nimport type { FrameReport } from '../gfx/renderer';\nx new: ok: 24 kinds: node x new <kind> <id>\naction: An action: a multi-beat sequence a body plays (beats of body states, a motion or a move, a clip, IK targets, a flip, a squash, events, flags, cancel windows), with a unit test that plays every beat to its end and passes its check through animCheckBody on a humanoid (PLAN.md WP 8.7). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/actions/<name> (its id is action:<name>): labs/box/actions/bow\nbody: A puppet body: parts on the humanoid skeleton's bones, one line each, with material slots and mirrored parts, with a unit test that compiles it for every build and draws it within 10 draw calls (PLAN.md WP 3.8). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/bodies/<name> (its id is body:<name>): labs/box/bodies/knight\nbuild: A body build: the humanoid skeleton's proportions in metres (limbs, torso, head, stance, gait), with a unit test that stands its rest pose on the floor and runs anim.check (PLAN.md WP 3.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/builds/<name> (its id is build:<name>): labs/box/builds/ogre\ncamera: A camera mode of a game's own (the kind camera): its code cam3=<name>[,…] and the camera it makes of its own pure steps (followCamera), with a unit test that its code makes it and gives it back, that it follows the player smoothly and settles on him, and that the camera rig blends into it (PLAN.md WP 13.8). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/cameras/<name>, the name a word no engine camera or view has (its code is cam3=<name>): labs/box/cameras/dolly\ncourse: A playground feel course (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.1, PLAN.md WP 13.4): recorded inputs on the gym from a start on its open floor, its numbers against §3.2's references with the body's and the cameras' bounds, and its unit test, which runs it and judges every number as x feel does. Id: the module's path, labs/playground/courses/<name> (its id is course:playground-<name>; node x feel labs/playground --course <name> runs it): labs/playground/courses/stairs\nemitter: An emitter: a particle effect as data (how its particles start, move and look; its burst, the event types it answers, its rate and light), with a unit test that its event bursts its particles within budget (PLAN.md WP 6.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/emitters/<name> (its id is emitter:<name>): labs/box/emitters/spores\nfeature: A rendering technique behind its feature (PLAN.md WP 11.1, ADR-0025 amendment 30): its feature entry and the advice code the registry raises while it is off, with a unit test that reads both through the registry; engine/gfx/features.ts registers it and hands the technique out only while it is on. Id: the module's path, engine/gfx/enhanced/<name> (its feature is feature:<name>, its advice GFX_NO_<NAME>): engine/gfx/enhanced/softShadows\ngeometry: A geometry: a shape props and levels name by id, built from the kit's builders, with a unit test that checks it by the geo QA family's measures (PLAN.md WP 2.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/geometry/<name> (its id is geometry:<name>): labs/box/geometry/crate\ninteractable: An interactable: what a player uses and strikes, as a component on a prop (the kinds usable and strikable: its prompt, what using it does, what a strike does) with its event and `add`, which stands one in a scene, with a unit test that a player before it sees its prompt and opens and shuts it with interact, and that a locked one stays shut until his punch bursts it open (PLAN.md WP 13.16). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/interactables/<name> (its component and its event are <name>, a word no other component has; a scene's setup stands one with <name>.add(w, { at })): labs/box/interactables/locker\nlevel: A level written as storeys (level format 2): its text in a module, with its rooms' markers and floors, a door, windows, a flight of stairs to the floor above, a railed stairwell and a gable roof, with a unit test that it compiles to its storeys and rooms, each room's marker inside it, and passes every check node x qa level runs: every spawn and room reached, doors wider than a body, headroom, flights walkable (PLAN.md WP 13.13). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/levels/<name> (its id is level:<name>; a scene names it, level: 'level:<name>'): labs/box/levels/cabin\nlook: A look: a material style plus a filter chain (post-processing), as data, with a unit test that it parses, posterizes with 0 or 8 and more levels, and that node x set post.look switches to it (PLAN.md WP 7.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/looks/<name> (its id is look:<name>; kebab-case, as the engine's looks are): labs/box/looks/ink-wash\nmaterial: A material: three.js's classic parameters as data (color, map, roughness, emissive, flags), drawn by the engine, with a unit test that compiles it (PLAN.md WP 2.3). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/materials/<name> (its id is material:<name>): labs/box/materials/mossyBrick\nmodule: A TypeScript module: its file comment, one documented export and a unit test beside it (PLAN.md §6.8). Id: the module's repository path without .ts, its name in camelCase: engine/core/clock\nmove: A move: a named strike in metres, radians and seconds (the swing's arc, height and reach, what strikes, the body motion, the phase times and the hit window), with a unit test that plays it on a timeline and measures its reach on a humanoid (PLAN.md WP 6.1). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/moves/<name> (its id is move:<name>): labs/box/moves/lunge\nmovement: A movement profile: how a character moves, as data (the character controller's settings it sets and the shooter model's numbers it changes, every other one Apex's), with a unit test that makes a character of it and measures its run, sprint and jump on the feel course against its own numbers (PLAN.md WP 13.3). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/movement/<name> (its id is movement:<name>; a character takes it with createCharacter({ movement: id })): labs/box/movement/heavy\nprop: A prop: a thing that stands in a level, as data (meshes by geometry and material id, a body, an optional light spot, where a level places it, hooks), with a unit test that its parts build clean and stand as tall as its body (PLAN.md WP 4.5). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/props/<name> (its id is prop:<name>): labs/box/props/chest\nrig: A procedural rig: a creature made of the blocks of engine/anim/proc (it starts as a small four-legged walker: a planted gait on two diagonals, two-bone legs, a body bobbing on a spring, a wagging tail, a looking head) and its rig spec, with a unit test that passes it through rigCheck and walks it on its diagonals (PLAN.md WP 10.1). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/rigs/<name> (its rig's id is rig:<name>; `node x qa anim` drives the fixture rigs, a game's own test drives its own): labs/box/rigs/crab\n… 7 more in out/new/all/output.txt\nreport: out/new/all/report.json\nx describe: ok: 33 kinds, 783 entries\naction (28): A multi-beat sequence played on a body: beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and events. The engine's set is data/...\nactionMap (4): Named actions and the physical codes bound to each (keys by KeyboardEvent.code, mouse, pad by position): what the input de...\nbody (2): Puppet bodies: parts on the humanoid skeleton, one line each, with material slots (the palette).\nbuild (6): Body proportions for the humanoid skeleton, in metres before size (limbs, torso, head, stance, gait).\nclipset (10): A set of readable clips loaded for playing (PLAN.md §10): its header and its clips. The library is data/anim/sets/, one dir...\ncomponent (44): Component kinds: the plain-object data entities hold (e.position), with their fields. The canonical state and the hash re...\ndevAction (5): Dev actions: changes made to a running scene between steps (god mode, freeze, spawn, travel, time scale), recorded in repl...\nemitter (27): Emitters: particle effects as data (how particles start, move and look), burst where something happens, answering event typ...\nfeature (13): Optional GPU features and techniques: what each needs, what is lost without it, and its advice code.\ngeometry (7): Shapes props, levels and bodies name by id: a builder of a three.js BufferGeometry with its parameters and a vertex budget.\nglyph (25): Level legend glyphs: what one map character means (heights, floor shape, solid, collider and mesh recipes, nav flags, spawn, ...\ninspectorMember (46): Members of the inspector (__engine, createHeadless): a call or property with its help line, argument schema and imp...\nlevel (0): Levels: a level text (front matter and a map in legend characters) that compileLevel turns into data.\nlight (1): What a level's light spot shines with: three.js's PointLight parameters, its priority in the light pool and its flicker.\nlook (144): Looks: a material style plus a filter chain (post-processing), and the sky that lights it, as whole looks or one-filter presets.\nmaterial (29): Materials as data with three.js's classic parameters (color, map, roughness, emissive, flags); the engine decides how to d...\nmove (25): A named strike: the swing (arc, height, reach, plane), what strikes, the body motion, the phase times and the hit window.\n… 17 more in out/describe/all/output.txt\nreport: out/describe/all/report.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"c5b404a3-e88c-49f7-9f76-36ea399fa627","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:35:46.331Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"data/anim:\nLICENSES\nSOURCES.md\nactions\ncatalogs\ncmu\nsets\nupgrade.json\n\ndata/anim/actions:\nbackflip.json\nbored.json\ncharge.json\ndeathBlown.json\ndeathCrush.json\ndeathPop.json\ndeathShock.json\ndeathShrink.json\ndeathSink.json\ndeathSpin.json\ndeathTopple.json\ndizzy.json\ngetUp.json\ngrab.json\ngrabbed.json\ngroundPound.json\nhurt.json\nitemGet.json\njump.json\nlandHeavy.json\nledgeHang.json\npant.json\nrise.json\nroll.json\nshiver.json\nspinJump.json\ntechRoll.json\nwallSlide.json\n\ndata/anim/sets:\ncmu\nmesh2motion\nquaternius\nstyle100\nstyle100_back\nstyle100_backrun\nstyle100_idle\nstyle100_run\nstyle100_side\nstyle100_siderun\n16: * `entities` and `get` → the world, read-only (a query names an undeclared component with the closest); `seed(n)`\n75:  seed: number;\n93:  seed(n?: number): number;\n178:    help: \"The engine's identity and health (version, runtime, adapter, features, downgrades, deps) and the run: scene, seed, tick, hz.\",\n188:      seed: h.session.world.seed,\n221:  seed: {\n222:    help: \"Starts the scene again with seed n (keeping the settings changed since it started) when n is given; returns the run's seed.\",\n224:      n: { type: 'integer', minimum: 0, description: 'The new seed: a whole number, 0 or more, as replays hold it.' },\n228:      return h.session.world.seed;\n/**\n * @file The engine without a page (PLAN.md §8.3, §6.9, WPs 1.6 and 2.7; doctrine: Verifiable): `createHeadless({\n * scene, seed, settings, view })` starts a scene as a recorded session (engine/sim/scene.ts) and returns its\n * inspector, the same object a page publishes as `window.__engine` minus a renderer: `render` and `shot` throw\n * `DEV_NO_RENDERER`. With `view: true` it also builds the scene's presentation graph in Node (engine/app/view.ts: the\n * stage and its camera, no GPU), so `scene.dump()` and `camera` work headless and view logic is tested in T1; the\n * scene's level must be registered first (`APP_NO_LEVEL`). `x sim` runs scenes through it, and tests drive the sim\n * with the same calls an agent makes in a page.\n *\n * Time: a headless engine has a frame clock over the run's settings, like a page, but no frames. `step(n, intents)`\n * runs n steps at once, inside the session, so the steps are recorded and `headlessHost(h).session.record()` is the run\n * as a replay. `pause`, `resume` and `timeScale` set the clock's settings as in a page (no real time passes headless,\n * so only `step` runs steps). After each step the run hands the hit-stop its rules asked for (engine/sim/damage.ts\n * `takeHitStop`) to its clock, whose budget shrinks stops that follow closely, so a page's frames stop and a crowd hit\n * stutters once; a page's real-time `?replay=` playback hands them over the same way (`stepped`), and a dev action's\n * goes with the next step, as in that playback. `seed(n)` starts the scene again with seed n, a new session and clock,\n * keeping every setting that differs from what a fresh start gives; `replay(text)` starts the replay's run (its\n * scene, seed and settings, keeping the view settings that differ, which never change play: a page's look stays) and\n * plays it, with a page's loop held, so it drops the stops it asks for (none is left to freeze a later frame). The\n * run's stats meter (engine/dev/stats.ts, `__engine.stats()`) times every step it runs, and the world's systems\n * through the world's probe; a page times its frames through it and gives it the renderer's counts (`Drawing.counts`).\n *\n * `createAppHost` is the run behind both engines: engine/app/engine.ts adds a page's drawing to it. Invariants:\n * everything runs synchronously after the promise resolves (the fdlibm swap holds only through synchronous entry\n * points); runs share nothing but the registry; the inspector holds only its members, so the run behind it is reached\n * through `headlessHost(h)` (tools only), whose `session` is what members get: the recorded operations and the world\n * read-only (engine/dev/members.ts). Its `restore` and `act` move entities between steps, so they snap the view to the\n * world (no interpolation from stale places), as a restart does. A seed is a whole number from 0 (`SIM_BAD_SEED`).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\n * import { defineScene } from '../sim/scene';\n * const demo = defineScene('demo', { setup: (w) => void w.spawn() }, createRegistry());\n * const h = await createHeadless({ scene: demo, seed: 3 });\n * h.step(60); // 60: the tick after the steps\n * h.entities(); // [{ id: 1, components: [] }]\n * @see engine/app/headless.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError, defineCodes, log as sharedLog, type Log } from '../core/log';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, sameValue, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { isPlainObject } from '../core/schema';\nimport { createSettings, defineSettings, type SettingValue } from '../core/settings';\nimport { createClock, TIME_SETTINGS, type Clock } from '../core/time';\nimport '../dev/actions'; // registers `__engine.actions` and `__engine.bot`\nimport { createInspector, type Inspector } from '../dev/inspector';\nimport { recordedRun, type InspectorHost, type RecordedRun } from '../dev/members';\nimport { createStatsMeter, type RenderCounts, type StatsMeter } from '../dev/stats';\nimport { createMaterialLibrary, type MaterialLibrary } from '../gfx/materials/library';\nimport type { FrameReport } from '../gfx/renderer';\nx new: ok: 24 kinds: node x new <kind> <id>\naction: An action: a multi-beat sequence a body plays (beats of body states, a motion or a move, a clip, IK targets, a flip, a squash, events, flags, cancel windows), with a unit test that plays every beat to its end and passes its check through animCheckBody on a humanoid (PLAN.md WP 8.7). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/actions/<name> (its id is action:<name>): labs/box/actions/bow\nbody: A puppet body: parts on the humanoid skeleton's bones, one line each, with material slots and mirrored parts, with a unit test that compiles it for every build and draws it within 10 draw calls (PLAN.md WP 3.8). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/bodies/<name> (its id is body:<name>): labs/box/bodies/knight\nbuild: A body build: the humanoid skeleton's proportions in metres (limbs, torso, head, stance, gait), with a unit test that stands its rest pose on the floor and runs anim.check (PLAN.md WP 3.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/builds/<name> (its id is build:<name>): labs/box/builds/ogre\ncamera: A camera mode of a game's own (the kind camera): its code cam3=<name>[,…] and the camera it makes of its own pure steps (followCamera), with a unit test that its code makes it and gives it back, that it follows the player smoothly and settles on him, and that the camera rig blends into it (PLAN.md WP 13.8). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/cameras/<name>, the name a word no engine camera or view has (its code is cam3=<name>): labs/box/cameras/dolly\ncourse: A playground feel course (docs/PLAYGROUND.md §9.1, PLAN.md WP 13.4): recorded inputs on the gym from a start on its open floor, its numbers against §3.2's references with the body's and the cameras' bounds, and its unit test, which runs it and judges every number as x feel does. Id: the module's path, labs/playground/courses/<name> (its id is course:playground-<name>; node x feel labs/playground --course <name> runs it): labs/playground/courses/stairs\nemitter: An emitter: a particle effect as data (how its particles start, move and look; its burst, the event types it answers, its rate and light), with a unit test that its event bursts its particles within budget (PLAN.md WP 6.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/emitters/<name> (its id is emitter:<name>): labs/box/emitters/spores\nfeature: A rendering technique behind its feature (PLAN.md WP 11.1, ADR-0025 amendment 30): its feature entry and the advice code the registry raises while it is off, with a unit test that reads both through the registry; engine/gfx/features.ts registers it and hands the technique out only while it is on. Id: the module's path, engine/gfx/enhanced/<name> (its feature is feature:<name>, its advice GFX_NO_<NAME>): engine/gfx/enhanced/softShadows\ngeometry: A geometry: a shape props and levels name by id, built from the kit's builders, with a unit test that checks it by the geo QA family's measures (PLAN.md WP 2.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/geometry/<name> (its id is geometry:<name>): labs/box/geometry/crate\ninteractable: An interactable: what a player uses and strikes, as a component on a prop (the kinds usable and strikable: its prompt, what using it does, what a strike does) with its event and `add`, which stands one in a scene, with a unit test that a player before it sees its prompt and opens and shuts it with interact, and that a locked one stays shut until his punch bursts it open (PLAN.md WP 13.16). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/interactables/<name> (its component and its event are <name>, a word no other component has; a scene's setup stands one with <name>.add(w, { at })): labs/box/interactables/locker\nlevel: A level written as storeys (level format 2): its text in a module, with its rooms' markers and floors, a door, windows, a flight of stairs to the floor above, a railed stairwell and a gable roof, with a unit test that it compiles to its storeys and rooms, each room's marker inside it, and passes every check node x qa level runs: every spawn and room reached, doors wider than a body, headroom, flights walkable (PLAN.md WP 13.13). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/levels/<name> (its id is level:<name>; a scene names it, level: 'level:<name>'): labs/box/levels/cabin\nlook: A look: a material style plus a filter chain (post-processing), as data, with a unit test that it parses, posterizes with 0 or 8 and more levels, and that node x set post.look switches to it (PLAN.md WP 7.4). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/looks/<name> (its id is look:<name>; kebab-case, as the engine's looks are): labs/box/looks/ink-wash\nmaterial: A material: three.js's classic parameters as data (color, map, roughness, emissive, flags), drawn by the engine, with a unit test that compiles it (PLAN.md WP 2.3). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/materials/<name> (its id is material:<name>): labs/box/materials/mossyBrick\nmodule: A TypeScript module: its file comment, one documented export and a unit test beside it (PLAN.md §6.8). Id: the module's repository path without .ts, its name in camelCase: engine/core/clock\nmove: A move: a named strike in metres, radians and seconds (the swing's arc, height and reach, what strikes, the body motion, the phase times and the hit window), with a unit test that plays it on a timeline and measures its reach on a humanoid (PLAN.md WP 6.1). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/moves/<name> (its id is move:<name>): labs/box/moves/lunge\nmovement: A movement profile: how a character moves, as data (the character controller's settings it sets and the shooter model's numbers it changes, every other one Apex's), with a unit test that makes a character of it and measures its run, sprint and jump on the feel course against its own numbers (PLAN.md WP 13.3). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/movement/<name> (its id is movement:<name>; a character takes it with createCharacter({ movement: id })): labs/box/movement/heavy\nprop: A prop: a thing that stands in a level, as data (meshes by geometry and material id, a body, an optional light spot, where a level places it, hooks), with a unit test that its parts build clean and stand as tall as its body (PLAN.md WP 4.5). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/props/<name> (its id is prop:<name>): labs/box/props/chest\nrig: A procedural rig: a creature made of the blocks of engine/anim/proc (it starts as a small four-legged walker: a planted gait on two diagonals, two-bone legs, a body bobbing on a spring, a wagging tail, a looking head) and its rig spec, with a unit test that passes it through rigCheck and walks it on its diagonals (PLAN.md WP 10.1). Id: the module's path, labs/<lab>/rigs/<name> (its rig's id is rig:<name>; `node x qa anim` drives the fixture rigs, a game's own test drives its own): labs/box/rigs/crab\n… 7 more in out/new/all/output.txt\nreport: out/new/all/report.json\nx describe: ok: 33 kinds, 783 entries\naction (28): A multi-beat sequence played on a body: beats of body states, clips, IK targets, flips and events. The engine's set is data/...\nactionMap (4): Named actions and the physical codes bound to each (keys by KeyboardEvent.code, mouse, pad by position): what the input de...\nbody (2): Puppet bodies: parts on the humanoid skeleton, one line each, with material slots (the palette).\nbuild (6): Body proportions for the humanoid skeleton, in metres before size (limbs, torso, head, stance, gait).\nclipset (10): A set of readable clips loaded for playing (PLAN.md §10): its header and its clips. The library is data/anim/sets/, one dir...\ncomponent (44): Component kinds: the plain-object data entities hold (e.position), with their fields. The canonical state and the hash re...\ndevAction (5): Dev actions: changes made to a running scene between steps (god mode, freeze, spawn, travel, time scale), recorded in repl...\nemitter (27): Emitters: particle effects as data (how particles start, move and look), burst where something happens, answering event typ...\nfeature (13): Optional GPU features and techniques: what each needs, what is lost without it, and its advice code.\ngeometry (7): Shapes props, levels and bodies name by id: a builder of a three.js BufferGeometry with its parameters and a vertex budget.\nglyph (25): Level legend glyphs: what one map character means (heights, floor shape, solid, collider and mesh recipes, nav flags, spawn, ...\ninspectorMember (46): Members of the inspector (__engine, createHeadless): a call or property with its help line, argument schema and imp...\nlevel (0): Levels: a level text (front matter and a map in legend characters) that compileLevel turns into data.\nlight (1): What a level's light spot shines with: three.js's PointLight parameters, its priority in the light pool and its flicker.\nlook (144): Looks: a material style plus a filter chain (post-processing), and the sky that lights it, as whole looks or one-filter presets.\nmaterial (29): Materials as data with three.js's classic parameters (color, map, roughness, emissive, flags); the engine decides how to d...\nmove (25): A named strike: the swing (arc, height, reach, plane), what strikes, the body motion, the phase times and the hit window.\n… 17 more in out/describe/all/output.txt\nreport: out/describe/all/report.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014q1t3SJVaSd5sGpuMZAWuP","permission_decision":{"deci