{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjAbXxLT1caRs8YHz2Wi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0197bpUz2d89Ubhj9Md6P1vC","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"R=/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-212002__x601dca/work/.research/ai-hub; sed -n 345,362p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 462,500p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 726,765p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 145,160p $R/docs/multiplayer-plan.md | cut -c1-240","description":"Read hub README sections on couch co-op, sessions and metadata"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8557,"cache_read_input_tokens":359550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8557},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"24035d22-8b46-4cfe-a6d6-e3d4be4353c7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:40:45.244Z","request_id":"req_011CgjAbXbVzky3Uc7MomtRQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0197bpUz2d89Ubhj9Md6P1vC":{"command":"R=/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-212002__x601dca/work/.research/ai-hub; sed -n 345,362p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 462,500p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 726,765p $R/README.md | cut -c1-240; sed -n 145,160p $R/docs/multiplayer-plan.md | cut -c1-240","description":"Read hub README sections on couch co-op, sessions and metadata"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0197bpUz2d89Ubhj9Md6P1vC","type":"tool_result","content":"  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n\nThe hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\ntime fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with\nits own colours, and its name, blurb, facts and keys sit over them. Under it\nrun the rails: *Jump back in*, *Your favourites*, *Fresh on the wall*, one per\n*Play with friends* (the games that play in a session), one per genre, one for\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\nown, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single pla\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\nimport that followed it.\ngame.\n\n| Version 2 | Now |\n|---|---|\n| 0 Watch | Parallel and ghost: the live scoreboard (*Now*). Shared worlds: a copy with no player of its own, fed the same traffic (*Later*) |\n| 1 Parallel | Mode `parallel`, client 1.3.0 (*Now*) |\n| 2 Ghosts | Mode `ghost`: parallel, plus the other players shown while they are in the same area, never touching a world (`race` until Nov 2) |\n| 3 Together | Mode `shared-world`: the host's copy runs the world (`versus` and `co-op` until Nov 2) |\n| 4 Natural multiplayer | A shared world, in whatever form the game's own multiplayer takes |\n| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n\n**One mode per game.** A list of modes per game would mean a code path and a\nconformance run per mode in every game, for a choice the hub never needs to\nmake. The lobby needs no new picker either: the deal alternates between the\nparallel and ghost games and the shared worlds, so a dozen shared worlds are\nnot buried under hundreds of the others.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"12553198-1a28-4fb9-95c2-290f981775a1","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:40:45.275Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n\nThe hub as a console's home screen, for a TV and a controller. One game at a\ntime fills the screen: its stills cross-fade behind everything, tinted with\nits own colours, and its name, blurb, facts and keys sit over them. Under it\nrun the rails: *Jump back in*, *Your favourites*, *Fresh on the wall*, one per\n*Play with friends* (the games that play in a session), one per genre, one for\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHere is the part worth knowing: **the filter rails are not declared anywhere.**\nThey are read off whatever the games actually carry. Fill in `model` on a few\ngames and a Model rail appears on the wall by itself; remove them and it goes.\nAny field works, including ones invented on the spot. A game can also carry its\nown, with `<meta name=\"hub:model\" content=\"…\">` in its head. A field whose\nvalues are nearly all different (a build id, a URL) is searchable but never\nbecomes a rail. Every field is searchable. `harness` is searched and shown in\nthe drawer but never gets a rail, and neither do `source` and `series`, which\nhave Original, Inspired and the stacks.\n\n`collection` comes from the folder a game sits in, but it is an ordinary field:\nan override, or a `hub:collection` meta, replaces it.\n\nThe library's own catalogue uses these fields, all in `data/overrides.json`:\n\n| Field | What it is | On the wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| `genre` | one of a fixed list (Racing, Platformer, Life Sim, Tech Demo…) | the Genre rail, and the cabinet's panel |\n| `type` | `Game`, `Tech demo` or `Tool` | the shelf: games, or tech demos & tools |\n| `tags` | 2–5 from a fixed vocabulary (`3d`, `retro`, `co-op`, `touch`…) | the Tags rail |\n| `players` | `1–2`, `1–4`… only when a game has co-op or versus | the Players rail: *Single player*, or *Multiplayer*, which drops a menu of head counts (*4 players* finds every game that seats four). A game with no `players` is single pla\n| `source` | the classic a game riffs on, as it is titled (`Dragon Quest IX`); none for an original | Original or Inspired, the stacks, the drawer, and search |\n| `series` | the franchise, only when it is not the source itself (`Dragon Quest` for `Dragon Quest IX`) | the stacks |\n| `controls` | a one-line summary | the drawer |\n| `description` | one or two plain sentences | the drawer |\n\nEvery game with the same `source` must have the same `series`; `pnpm test`\nchecks. A game can name its own source with `<meta name=\"hub:source\">`\n(`hub:inspired-by`, the older name, still works). Source leads the rail strip.\nGenre, Collection, Type and Players follow it when they exist; the\nrest follow by how many games carry them. `docs/curation-2026-09-24.md`\nrecords how the library was curated: which file of each game was kept, and\nwhich older iterations it replaced. `docs/curation-2026-09-28.md` records the\nimport from the source-named GPT-games folder and maps the old slugs to the\ncurrent ones; `docs/curation-2026-09-28-new-ai-stuff.md` records the smaller\nimport that followed it.\ngame.\n\n| Version 2 | Now |\n|---|---|\n| 0 Watch | Parallel and ghost: the live scoreboard (*Now*). Shared worlds: a copy with no player of its own, fed the same traffic (*Later*) |\n| 1 Parallel | Mode `parallel`, client 1.3.0 (*Now*) |\n| 2 Ghosts | Mode `ghost`: parallel, plus the other players shown while they are in the same area, never touching a world (`race` until Nov 2) |\n| 3 Together | Mode `shared-world`: the host's copy runs the world (`versus` and `co-op` until Nov 2) |\n| 4 Natural multiplayer | A shared world, in whatever form the game's own multiplayer takes |\n| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n\n**One mode per game.** A list of modes per game would mean a code path and a\nconformance run per mode in every game, for a choice the hub never needs to\nmake. The lobby needs no new picker either: the deal alternates between the\nparallel and ghost games and the shared worlds, so a dozen shared worlds are\nnot buried under hundreds of the others.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0197bpUz2d89Ubhj9Md6P1vC","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjAbXxLT1caRs8YHz2Wi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AR2H4YE63SXzvgaPzLqLFT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-212002__x601dca/work/.research/proto && sed -n 1,70p engine/gfx/weapons.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,70p engine/world/weapons.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,60p engine/sim/damage.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,60p engine/sim/interact.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,45p engine/sim/reactions.ts | cut -c1-200","description":"Read weapon effects, damage, interaction and reaction modules"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8557,"cache_read_input_tokens":359550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8557},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"5e16667e-724c-44cb-84db-ad7c4dd95391","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:40:47.697Z","request_id":"req_011CgjAbXbVzky3Uc7MomtRQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AR2H4YE63SXzvgaPzLqLFT":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-212002__x601dca/work/.research/proto && sed -n 1,70p engine/gfx/weapons.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,70p engine/world/weapons.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,60p engine/sim/damage.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,60p engine/sim/interact.ts | cut -c1-200; echo ...; sed -n 1,45p engine/sim/reactions.ts | cut -c1-200","description":"Read weapon effects, damage, interaction and reaction modules"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AR2H4YE63SXzvgaPzLqLFT","type":"tool_result","content":"/**\n * @file Firearms drawn (PLAN.md WP 13.20; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.2, §4): what the view shows of the weapons the sim\n * fires (engine/sim/weapons.ts; the kind `weapon`, engine/world/weapons.ts), presentation only.\n * - **The gun in the hand.** `weaponOut(entity)` is the weapon an actor's component `weapons` has out (null:\n *   holstered, or none), and `gunPose(skeleton, world, barrel)` where its prop stands: at the right hand's grip (the\n *   socket `weaponR`), its +Z along the hold's barrel (engine/anim/traversal/hold.ts `HoldPlant.barrel`), its +Y as\n *   near up as that leaves. The stage draws it as its prop (engine/gfx/stageProps.ts `carry`), first person as a mesh\n *   of its own at the arms' grip, by their lens (engine/gfx/viewmodel/body.ts): `gunGeometry`, its prop's parts as one\n *   geometry coloured by their tints, which the arms' vertex-coloured material draws; `muzzlePoint` is where its shots\n *   leave.\n * - **Shots** (`createWeaponFx`): `feed(trace, muzzleOf)` plays each `gunshot` event of the world's trace once: a\n *   tracer from the muzzle (`muzzleOf(id)`, the carried gun's, else `TRACER.ahead` along the shot from where it left)\n *   to where it ended, for `TRACER.life`; a flash of sparks at the muzzle, and where it struck a star and sparks off\n *   the surface (the effects' `emitter:impact` and `emitter:sparks`) and a hole facing its normal (`HOLES`), the latest\n *   `HOLES.capacity` kept. `frame(dt)` moves the tracers on; `register(warmup)` makes both batches' pipelines before\n *   the first frame.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only: it reads the trace and the posed bodies and writes nothing back; each event plays\n * once, by its number, as the effects' own `feed` reads the trace; its batches exist from the start and never grow.\n *\n * Carried from the hit effects of `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:1569-1580` (`hitFx`: a star and sparks where a blow\n * lands), with Apex Legends' tracers and bullet holes.\n *\n * @example\n * import { Vector3 } from 'three/webgpu';\n * import { Animator } from '../anim/character';\n * // const shots = createWeaponFx({ effects: stage.fx.effects }); stage.scene.add(shots.root);\n * // shots.feed(world.events.trace(), (id) => stage.actors.muzzle(id)); shots.frame(dt); // each drawn frame\n * const { skeleton, world } = new Animator({ build: 'build:adult', clock: 1 }).update({ position: new Vector3() }).body;\n * gunPose(skeleton, world, new Vector3(0, 0, 1)).position; // the right hand's grip, the barrel along +Z\n * @see engine/gfx/weapons.test.ts\n */\nimport {\n  BoxGeometry,\n  BufferAttribute,\n  CustomBlending,\n  Matrix4,\n  MeshBasicNodeMaterial,\n  OneFactor,\n  OneMinusSrcAlphaFactor,\n  PlaneGeometry,\n  Quaternion,\n  Vector3,\n  type BufferGeometry,\n  type Group,\n} from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { socketPosition, type WorldPose } from '../anim/fk';\nimport type { Skeleton } from '../anim/skeleton';\nimport { toLinear } from '../core/color';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport type { EntityData } from '../sim/state';\nimport { getProp } from '../world/props/prop';\nimport { getWeapon, type WeaponKind } from '../world/weapons';\nimport type { Effects } from './fx/effects';\nimport { mergeGeometries } from './geometry/groups';\nimport { createInstancedBatch, type InstancedBatch } from './instancing';\nimport { getMaterial } from './materials/material';\nimport { buildPropPart, partMatrix } from './props';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A tracer's look: its life (s), width (m), colour, and how far ahead of where a shot left it starts without a gun. */\nexport const TRACER = Object.freeze({ life: 0.05, width: 0.012, color: '#ffd27a', ahead: 0.8, capacity: 32 });\n/** A bullet hole: its size (m), colour and how far off its surface (m); the latest `capacity` stay. */\nexport const HOLES = Object.freeze({ size: 0.05, color: '#14110f', lift: 0.004, capacity: 64 });\n\n/** A gun in a hand as the view draws it: its weapon, and where its grip stands and its turn (world). */\nexport interface HeldGun {\n  readonly kind: WeaponKind;\n  readonly position: Vector3;\n  readonly rotation: Quaternion;\n...\n/**\n * @file Weapons as data (PLAN.md WP 13.20; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.2; doctrine: Common ground): the registry kind\n * `weapon`, a firearm's numbers in plain terms (`defineWeapon(id, spec)`): how fast it fires (`rate`) and whether it\n * fires on while the trigger is held (`auto`), the damage a shot takes from a person and how far it reaches, the cone\n * its shots stray in (`spread`: from the hip, more on the move, little down the sights), its recoil (`recoil`: a\n * kick a shot, [up, right] degrees, the burst's aim climbing by their sum, the last repeating; `settle` seconds after\n * the last shot the burst ends and its kick falls away, `recover` its time constant), its magazine and its two\n * reloads (a tactical one, and one from empty), its sights' lens\n * (`zoom`, at Apex's 70° field) and how fast they come up, how long it takes to draw and put away, where its shots\n * leave it (`muzzle`, in its frame), the prop it is drawn as (`prop`: its parts in its frame, +Z along the barrel\n * from the grip, +Y up, so `node x gallery prop` shows it) and the sound of its shot (`sound`, an `sfx` entry a game's\n * sound map plays for its `gunshot`, WP 13.22). engine/sim/weapons.ts fires them, engine/gfx/weapons.ts draws them,\n * and `recoilStep` and `spreadOf` are the numbers both read.\n *\n * Invariants: data and pure functions only; angles in degrees as the fields say, seconds and metres. Problems raise\n * `CORE_BAD_SPEC`; a missing id gets `weapon:gun` after a warning.\n *\n * Carried as a concept from Apex Legends' weapons (a fire rate, a hip and a sights cone, a recoil pattern, a\n * magazine and two reloads, the sights' zoom, holstering's speed).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\n * const pistol = defineWeapon('weapon:pistol', { rate: 6, damage: 18, magazine: 12, recoil: [[1.5, 0.1]] }, createRegistry());\n * recoilStep(pistol, 4); // [1.5, 0.1]: the fifth shot's kick, the last step repeating\n * spreadOf(pistol, 1, false); // 0.15: down the sights, still\n * @see engine/world/weapons.test.ts\n */\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { defineSchema } from '../core/schema';\n\n/** A number field of a weapon (see `WEAPON_FIELDS`). */\nconst num = (value: number, minimum: number, unit: string, description: string) => ({ type: 'number', default: value, minimum, unit, description }) as const; // prettier-ignore\n\n/** A `weapon` kind's fields (see the file comment). */\nexport const WEAPON_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  rate: num(5, 0.1, 'shots/s', 'How fast it fires.'),\n  auto: { type: 'boolean', default: false, description: 'Whether it fires on while the trigger is held.' },\n  damage: num(20, 0, 'hp', 'Hit points a shot takes from a person.'),\n  range: num(100, 1, 'm', 'How far its shots reach.'),\n  spread: { type: 'object', description: 'The cone its shots stray in, half-angles (deg).', default: { hip: 2, move: 1.5, ads: 0.15 }, properties: { hip: num(2, 0, 'deg', 'From the hip.'), move: num(1\n  recoil: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' } }, default: [[0.5, 0]], unit: 'deg', description: \"Each shot's kick in a burst, [up, right]; the last repeats.\" }, // prett\n  settle: num(0.3, 0, 's', 'How long after its last shot a burst ends and its kick begins to fall away.'),\n  recover: num(0.15, 0, 's', 'The time constant its kick falls away in once its burst ends (0: at once).'),\n  magazine: { type: 'integer', default: 12, minimum: 1, description: 'Rounds a magazine holds.' },\n  reload: { type: 'object', description: 'How long a reload takes (s).', default: { tactical: 1.25, empty: 1.5 }, properties: { tactical: num(1.25, 0, 's', 'With rounds left.'), empty: num(1.5, 0, 's'\n  zoom: num(60, 1, 'deg', \"The sights' lens at a 70° field of view (Apex's): how far aiming down them zooms.\"),\n  sights: num(0.2, 0, 's', 'How long the sights take to come up.'),\n  draw: num(0.3, 0, 's', 'How long it takes to draw, and to put away.'),\n  muzzle: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: [0, 0.06, 0.3], unit: 'm', description: 'Where shots leave it, in its frame: +Z along the barrel from the grip, +Y up.' }, // prettier-ig\n  prop: { type: 'string', default: '', description: 'The prop it is drawn as, in its frame (\"\": none).' },\n  sound: { type: 'string', default: 'sfx:pistolShot', description: 'The sound of its shot, an sfx entry (the sim never plays it).' }, // prettier-ignore\n});\n\n/** A `weapon` entry (see the file comment). */\nexport type WeaponKind = Omit<Entry<typeof WEAPON_FIELDS>, 'recoil' | 'muzzle'> & {\n  readonly recoil: readonly (readonly [number, number])[];\n  readonly muzzle: readonly [number, number, number];\n};\n\n/** What `defineWeapon` takes: any of the kind's fields (`spread` and `reload` field by field), the rest at their defaults. */ // prettier-ignore\nexport type WeaponSpec = Partial<Omit<WeaponKind, 'id' | 'kind' | 'spread' | 'reload'>> & {\n  spread?: Partial<WeaponKind['spread']>;\n  reload?: Partial<WeaponKind['reload']>;\n};\n\n/** The problems of a weapon beyond its fields: a recoil of [up, right] pairs, a muzzle of three numbers. */\nfunction weaponProblems(kind: WeaponKind): string[] {\n  const problems: string[] = [];\n  if (!kind.recoil.length || !kind.recoil.every((k) => k.length === 2 && k.every(Number.isFinite))) problems.push(`recoil ${JSON.stringify(kind.recoil)} is not a list of [up, right] degrees`); // pret\n  if (kind.muzzle.length !== 3 || !kind.muzzle.every(Number.isFinite)) problems.push(`muzzle ${JSON.stringify(kind.muzzle)} is not [x, y, z] in metres`); // prettier-ignore\n...\n/**\n * @file Damage (PLAN.md WP 6.3, §6.4): what a hit does once the rules have decided it, generic and without formulas,\n * stats or balance: `damage(w, target, hit)` takes `hit.amount` from the target's health, pushes its crowd body\n * away from the blow as momentum the solver carries on, throws it up, kills it into the corpse group with the kill\n * launch, asks for a hit-stop and emits the event `damage` for the view (numbers, sparks, sounds). How much a hit\n * takes and how hard it pushes are the game's numbers, passed in.\n *\n * - **Health**: the game keeps it in a component (`HEALTH_FIELDS`): hit points, and when and by whom it was last hit,\n *   which flinches, stuns and flashes read (`w.tick - health.hitAt`). At 0 or below it is dead.\n * - **Bodies**: a crowd agent's (engine/physics/crowd.ts): `knockback` adds the push the same for every mass and the\n *   solver carries it into the crowd; `launch` throws it up; `killAgent` moves it to the group `corpse`, which the\n *   crowd walks through, and `updateCrowd` removes it later.\n * - **Hit-stop**: a sim decision the frame clock carries out (engine/core/time.ts). `requestHitStop` keeps the longest\n *   stop asked in the step (they never add up); the run's host takes it after the step (`takeHitStop`) for\n *   `clock.hitStop`, whose leaky budget shrinks stops that follow closely, so a crowd hit stops time once however many\n *   it strikes. The clock is outside the hash: a stop changes how many steps a frame runs, never what a step does.\n *\n * Invariants: a dead or absent target (health at 0 or below, a body no longer `alive`), or an entity holding the\n * component `invulnerable` (god mode: engine/sim/devActions.ts), takes no hit: `damage` returns null and changes nothing. Everything a hit changes is in the target's components and its body, and the \n * plain data, so a capture restores it. The asked stop waits in a side table by world, an outbox the sim never reads\n * (no listener: a listener would join the world's captured schedule), so it never changes the hash. Only hosts take it:\n * engine/sim-api.ts leaves `takeHitStop` out. No clock, no random stream: a rule that wants randomness draws from a\n * named stream and passes the result in.\n *\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:324-342` (`damageEnemy`: the knockback along the blow, the big\n * hit's launch, the kill launch and its 1.3 carry, the switch to the dead group, the freeze), the hit-stop through\n * the budget of `src/emberdeep/10-combat.js:45-76` (engine/core/time.ts).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createPhysics } from '../physics/world';\n * import { attachPhysics } from '../physics/binding';\n * import { createWorld } from './world';\n * const w = createWorld({ seed: 1 });\n * attachPhysics(w, createPhysics()); // a scene with physics: true has this\n * const health = { ...HEALTH, hp: 3 };\n * damage(w, { id: 4, health }, { amount: 5, from: [0, 0, 0], stop: 0.06 }); // { killed: true, hp: -2, … }; an agent's body is also knocked\n * takeHitStop(w); // 0.06: the host hands it to its frame clock (clock.hitStop), once\n * takeHitStop(w); // 0: taken\n * @see engine/sim/damage.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\nimport { copyValue, defineSchema, freezeValue, isPlainObject, show } from '../core/schema';\nimport { killAgent, knockback, launch, type CrowdAgent } from '../physics/crowd';\nimport type { Vec3Like } from '../physics/queries';\nimport { physicsOf } from '../physics/binding';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\n/** A target's health, for a scene's component: `defineComponent('health', { description, fields: HEALTH_FIELDS })`. */\nexport const HEALTH_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  hp: { type: 'number', default: 1, description: 'Hit points left: at 0 or below, it is dead.' },\n  hitAt: {\n    type: 'integer',\n    default: -1,\n    minimum: -1,\n    description: 'The step it was last hit (w.tick; −1: never): flinches, stuns and flashes count from it.',\n  },\n  hitBy: { type: 'integer', default: -1, minimum: -1, description: 'Who hit it last (an entity id; −1: unknown).' },\n});\n\n...\n/**\n * @file Use (PLAN.md WP 13.16; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.1): what a player's `interact` press uses, and what a strike does.\n * `addInteraction(w)` (a scene's setup, once) adds the system `interact` (phase `intents`, before the actors move and\n * before the doors, the panes and the switches, phase `anim`; the dev action `freeze` stops none of them). Each step\n * it finds, for every player actor, the usable thing in reach (`USE_REACH`, 2 m from its eye) and in view: along its\n * crosshair when the step has a `look` and an `aim` (first person and the shooter), the first body the eye's ray toward\n * the aim strikes, or a bodiless thing (a switch) it passes within `BODILESS` of before that; else the nearest within\n * 60° of where it faces, a wall, a door or a pane between hiding one. Only what can be used now counts (a sliding door\n * hides no switch). It keeps that thing and its prompt in the actor's `interactor` component, which the page shows with\n * the binding of the device used last.\n *\n * Usable things are asked of their entries, never their ids: `defineUsable(component, { prompt, use })` says how an\n * entity holding that component is used (a door: engine/sim/doors.ts; a switch: switches.ts), and a prop is usable when\n * its own `use` has a `prompt`, or when a player can carry it (dynamic, at most `CARRY_MASS`). Its `use` hook gets the\n * world, the entity and the user. A carried prop's body is driven 0.8 m in front of the eye each step (it still\n * collides, and drops when held back past the reach and 1.5 m); the next use throws it along the view at `THROW_SPEED`.\n * A strike (`strikeEntity`: a melee hit, engine/sim/melee.ts; a shot; a blast) lands on what takes one, the kind\n * `strikable` by component (`defineStrikable(component, strike, move?)`: a door bursts open, then breaks; a breakable\n * prop takes a point of damage; a thing a point), else a dynamic prop is knocked; a lamp or a switch takes none. The\n * entry may name the move a melee plays on it (a door's kick: `strikeEntry`).\n *\n * A use takes its press: `interactor.usedAt` is the step, and the actors' input leaves it out of the rides'\n * (engine/sim/actorInput.ts), so using a door beside a zipline does not ride it. Events: `use` `{ id, target }`,\n * `pickup` and `throw` (the same).\n *\n * Invariants: everything is in components (the interactor, the props', the doors'); candidates in id order, the\n * nearest first among them, ties to the lower id; the queries read the physics world as of the last step; a body's prop\n * is found by a scan of the props (`propOfBody`), never an index kept beside them.\n * @see engine/sim/interact.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { pressed, type Intents } from '../input/intents';\nimport { physicsOf } from '../physics/binding';\nimport { getProp, type Prop } from '../world/props/prop';\nimport type { ActorComponents } from './actors';\nimport { hitProp, propOfBody, type ScenePropComponents } from './props';\nimport { defineComponent } from './state';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\n/** How far from the eye a thing is in reach (m). */\nexport const USE_REACH = 2;\n/** The heaviest prop a player carries (kg): a chair, a box, a crate. */\nexport const CARRY_MASS = 25;\n/** How fast a carried prop leaves when it is thrown (m/s). */\nexport const THROW_SPEED = 8;\n/** The eye over the feet, standing and crouched (m): the cameras' (engine/gfx/cameras/effects.ts); a gun aims from it. */\nexport const EYE = { stand: 1.52, crouch: 0.81 } as const;\n/** How far in front of the eye a carried prop is held (m), and how much farther than the reach it may lag before it drops. */\nconst HOLD = { ahead: 0.8, slip: 1.5 } as const;\n/** The cosine of the widest angle from where a player faces that is \"in front\" (60°), written out: no Math at import. */\nconst FRONT = 0.5;\n/** How near a bodiless thing (a switch on a wall) the crosshair's ray must pass to be on it (m). */\nexport const BODILESS = 0.15;\n\n/** How an entity holding a component is used: what the HUD says (null: not now) and what using it does. */\nexport interface UsableSpec {\n  prompt(w: World, id: number, user: number): string | null;\n  use(w: World, id: number, user: number): void;\n...\n/**\n * @file Reactions and secondary motion on a scene's bodies (PLAN.md WP 6.2, WP 7.8, ADR-0018 amendment 1): the two\n * components that put engine/anim's reactions and secondary motion on an entity.\n *\n * - **`reactions`** (`REACTION_FIELDS`): a body's reactions (engine/anim/reactions.ts `Reactions`: the flinch spring,\n *   the knockdown, the death and the get-up chosen by how it lies) as the plain data `Reactions.save()` writes, so\n *   the hash, captures and replays hold them. `reactTo(w, id, act)` runs `act` on an entity's reactions, adding the\n *   component at rest the first time, and saves them back. `stepReactions(state, dt)` steps them one tick and returns\n *   what the phase playing tells the body (`down`, `pose`), to lay over its body states. The system `actors.anim`\n *   steps an actor's (engine/sim/actors.ts); game code steps any other body's in its own rig system (a crowd body's\n *   states, engine/sim/crowdBodies.ts). The view draws an actor's flinch and how it lies (engine/gfx/puppets/actors.ts).\n * - **`secondary`** (`SECONDARY_FIELDS`): the secondary motion the view hangs on an actor's puppet (engine/anim/\n *   secondary.ts `Secondary`: hair, a cape, a tail) and its colour. The sim only carries it, as it carries a build or\n *   a stand-in's material: the view steps the chains, so none of their motion reaches the hash.\n *\n * Invariants: everything a reaction remembers is in its component (the `Reactions` here are scratch, loaded at every\n * call), so a capture restores it exactly; a body at rest (no flinch, nothing playing) steps for nothing.\n *\n * @example\n * import { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\n * import { createWorld } from './world';\n * const w = createWorld({ seed: 1 });\n * const id = w.spawn({});\n * reactTo(w, id, (r) => r.knockDown(new Vector3(0, 0, -1), 0)); // struck from the front: it falls onto its back\n * stepReactions(w.get(id).reactions, w.dt); // { down: 1 }: what the body is told while it falls\n * @see engine/sim/reactions.test.ts\n */\nimport { REACTIONS, Reactions, type ReactionBody, type ReactionState } from '../anim/reactions';\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { defineSchema } from '../core/schema';\nimport { SIM_DT } from '../core/time';\nimport { defineComponent } from './state';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\nconst AT_REST: ReactionState = new Reactions().save();\n\n/** The component `reactions`' fields: `Reactions.save()`'s, at rest by default. */\nexport const REACTION_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  flinch: {\n    type: 'array',\n    items: { type: 'number' },\n    default: AT_REST.flinch,\n    description: 'The flinch: its lean right and forward (tilts’ tangents), then their speeds per second.',","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"96ea0c22-3e0d-4e48-9fae-139c23cba576","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:40:47.748Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/**\n * @file Firearms drawn (PLAN.md WP 13.20; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.2, §4): what the view shows of the weapons the sim\n * fires (engine/sim/weapons.ts; the kind `weapon`, engine/world/weapons.ts), presentation only.\n * - **The gun in the hand.** `weaponOut(entity)` is the weapon an actor's component `weapons` has out (null:\n *   holstered, or none), and `gunPose(skeleton, world, barrel)` where its prop stands: at the right hand's grip (the\n *   socket `weaponR`), its +Z along the hold's barrel (engine/anim/traversal/hold.ts `HoldPlant.barrel`), its +Y as\n *   near up as that leaves. The stage draws it as its prop (engine/gfx/stageProps.ts `carry`), first person as a mesh\n *   of its own at the arms' grip, by their lens (engine/gfx/viewmodel/body.ts): `gunGeometry`, its prop's parts as one\n *   geometry coloured by their tints, which the arms' vertex-coloured material draws; `muzzlePoint` is where its shots\n *   leave.\n * - **Shots** (`createWeaponFx`): `feed(trace, muzzleOf)` plays each `gunshot` event of the world's trace once: a\n *   tracer from the muzzle (`muzzleOf(id)`, the carried gun's, else `TRACER.ahead` along the shot from where it left)\n *   to where it ended, for `TRACER.life`; a flash of sparks at the muzzle, and where it struck a star and sparks off\n *   the surface (the effects' `emitter:impact` and `emitter:sparks`) and a hole facing its normal (`HOLES`), the latest\n *   `HOLES.capacity` kept. `frame(dt)` moves the tracers on; `register(warmup)` makes both batches' pipelines before\n *   the first frame.\n *\n * Invariants: presentation only: it reads the trace and the posed bodies and writes nothing back; each event plays\n * once, by its number, as the effects' own `feed` reads the trace; its batches exist from the start and never grow.\n *\n * Carried from the hit effects of `my-3d2dge:engine/my-3d2dge.js:1569-1580` (`hitFx`: a star and sparks where a blow\n * lands), with Apex Legends' tracers and bullet holes.\n *\n * @example\n * import { Vector3 } from 'three/webgpu';\n * import { Animator } from '../anim/character';\n * // const shots = createWeaponFx({ effects: stage.fx.effects }); stage.scene.add(shots.root);\n * // shots.feed(world.events.trace(), (id) => stage.actors.muzzle(id)); shots.frame(dt); // each drawn frame\n * const { skeleton, world } = new Animator({ build: 'build:adult', clock: 1 }).update({ position: new Vector3() }).body;\n * gunPose(skeleton, world, new Vector3(0, 0, 1)).position; // the right hand's grip, the barrel along +Z\n * @see engine/gfx/weapons.test.ts\n */\nimport {\n  BoxGeometry,\n  BufferAttribute,\n  CustomBlending,\n  Matrix4,\n  MeshBasicNodeMaterial,\n  OneFactor,\n  OneMinusSrcAlphaFactor,\n  PlaneGeometry,\n  Quaternion,\n  Vector3,\n  type BufferGeometry,\n  type Group,\n} from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { socketPosition, type WorldPose } from '../anim/fk';\nimport type { Skeleton } from '../anim/skeleton';\nimport { toLinear } from '../core/color';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport type { EntityData } from '../sim/state';\nimport { getProp } from '../world/props/prop';\nimport { getWeapon, type WeaponKind } from '../world/weapons';\nimport type { Effects } from './fx/effects';\nimport { mergeGeometries } from './geometry/groups';\nimport { createInstancedBatch, type InstancedBatch } from './instancing';\nimport { getMaterial } from './materials/material';\nimport { buildPropPart, partMatrix } from './props';\nimport type { Warmup } from './warmup';\n\n/** A tracer's look: its life (s), width (m), colour, and how far ahead of where a shot left it starts without a gun. */\nexport const TRACER = Object.freeze({ life: 0.05, width: 0.012, color: '#ffd27a', ahead: 0.8, capacity: 32 });\n/** A bullet hole: its size (m), colour and how far off its surface (m); the latest `capacity` stay. */\nexport const HOLES = Object.freeze({ size: 0.05, color: '#14110f', lift: 0.004, capacity: 64 });\n\n/** A gun in a hand as the view draws it: its weapon, and where its grip stands and its turn (world). */\nexport interface HeldGun {\n  readonly kind: WeaponKind;\n  readonly position: Vector3;\n  readonly rotation: Quaternion;\n...\n/**\n * @file Weapons as data (PLAN.md WP 13.20; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.2; doctrine: Common ground): the registry kind\n * `weapon`, a firearm's numbers in plain terms (`defineWeapon(id, spec)`): how fast it fires (`rate`) and whether it\n * fires on while the trigger is held (`auto`), the damage a shot takes from a person and how far it reaches, the cone\n * its shots stray in (`spread`: from the hip, more on the move, little down the sights), its recoil (`recoil`: a\n * kick a shot, [up, right] degrees, the burst's aim climbing by their sum, the last repeating; `settle` seconds after\n * the last shot the burst ends and its kick falls away, `recover` its time constant), its magazine and its two\n * reloads (a tactical one, and one from empty), its sights' lens\n * (`zoom`, at Apex's 70° field) and how fast they come up, how long it takes to draw and put away, where its shots\n * leave it (`muzzle`, in its frame), the prop it is drawn as (`prop`: its parts in its frame, +Z along the barrel\n * from the grip, +Y up, so `node x gallery prop` shows it) and the sound of its shot (`sound`, an `sfx` entry a game's\n * sound map plays for its `gunshot`, WP 13.22). engine/sim/weapons.ts fires them, engine/gfx/weapons.ts draws them,\n * and `recoilStep` and `spreadOf` are the numbers both read.\n *\n * Invariants: data and pure functions only; angles in degrees as the fields say, seconds and metres. Problems raise\n * `CORE_BAD_SPEC`; a missing id gets `weapon:gun` after a warning.\n *\n * Carried as a concept from Apex Legends' weapons (a fire rate, a hip and a sights cone, a recoil pattern, a\n * magazine and two reloads, the sights' zoom, holstering's speed).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createRegistry } from '../core/registry';\n * const pistol = defineWeapon('weapon:pistol', { rate: 6, damage: 18, magazine: 12, recoil: [[1.5, 0.1]] }, createRegistry());\n * recoilStep(pistol, 4); // [1.5, 0.1]: the fifth shot's kick, the last step repeating\n * spreadOf(pistol, 1, false); // 0.15: down the sights, still\n * @see engine/world/weapons.test.ts\n */\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Entry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { defineSchema } from '../core/schema';\n\n/** A number field of a weapon (see `WEAPON_FIELDS`). */\nconst num = (value: number, minimum: number, unit: string, description: string) => ({ type: 'number', default: value, minimum, unit, description }) as const; // prettier-ignore\n\n/** A `weapon` kind's fields (see the file comment). */\nexport const WEAPON_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  rate: num(5, 0.1, 'shots/s', 'How fast it fires.'),\n  auto: { type: 'boolean', default: false, description: 'Whether it fires on while the trigger is held.' },\n  damage: num(20, 0, 'hp', 'Hit points a shot takes from a person.'),\n  range: num(100, 1, 'm', 'How far its shots reach.'),\n  spread: { type: 'object', description: 'The cone its shots stray in, half-angles (deg).', default: { hip: 2, move: 1.5, ads: 0.15 }, properties: { hip: num(2, 0, 'deg', 'From the hip.'), move: num(1\n  recoil: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' } }, default: [[0.5, 0]], unit: 'deg', description: \"Each shot's kick in a burst, [up, right]; the last repeats.\" }, // prett\n  settle: num(0.3, 0, 's', 'How long after its last shot a burst ends and its kick begins to fall away.'),\n  recover: num(0.15, 0, 's', 'The time constant its kick falls away in once its burst ends (0: at once).'),\n  magazine: { type: 'integer', default: 12, minimum: 1, description: 'Rounds a magazine holds.' },\n  reload: { type: 'object', description: 'How long a reload takes (s).', default: { tactical: 1.25, empty: 1.5 }, properties: { tactical: num(1.25, 0, 's', 'With rounds left.'), empty: num(1.5, 0, 's'\n  zoom: num(60, 1, 'deg', \"The sights' lens at a 70° field of view (Apex's): how far aiming down them zooms.\"),\n  sights: num(0.2, 0, 's', 'How long the sights take to come up.'),\n  draw: num(0.3, 0, 's', 'How long it takes to draw, and to put away.'),\n  muzzle: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'number' }, default: [0, 0.06, 0.3], unit: 'm', description: 'Where shots leave it, in its frame: +Z along the barrel from the grip, +Y up.' }, // prettier-ig\n  prop: { type: 'string', default: '', description: 'The prop it is drawn as, in its frame (\"\": none).' },\n  sound: { type: 'string', default: 'sfx:pistolShot', description: 'The sound of its shot, an sfx entry (the sim never plays it).' }, // prettier-ignore\n});\n\n/** A `weapon` entry (see the file comment). */\nexport type WeaponKind = Omit<Entry<typeof WEAPON_FIELDS>, 'recoil' | 'muzzle'> & {\n  readonly recoil: readonly (readonly [number, number])[];\n  readonly muzzle: readonly [number, number, number];\n};\n\n/** What `defineWeapon` takes: any of the kind's fields (`spread` and `reload` field by field), the rest at their defaults. */ // prettier-ignore\nexport type WeaponSpec = Partial<Omit<WeaponKind, 'id' | 'kind' | 'spread' | 'reload'>> & {\n  spread?: Partial<WeaponKind['spread']>;\n  reload?: Partial<WeaponKind['reload']>;\n};\n\n/** The problems of a weapon beyond its fields: a recoil of [up, right] pairs, a muzzle of three numbers. */\nfunction weaponProblems(kind: WeaponKind): string[] {\n  const problems: string[] = [];\n  if (!kind.recoil.length || !kind.recoil.every((k) => k.length === 2 && k.every(Number.isFinite))) problems.push(`recoil ${JSON.stringify(kind.recoil)} is not a list of [up, right] degrees`); // pret\n  if (kind.muzzle.length !== 3 || !kind.muzzle.every(Number.isFinite)) problems.push(`muzzle ${JSON.stringify(kind.muzzle)} is not [x, y, z] in metres`); // prettier-ignore\n...\n/**\n * @file Damage (PLAN.md WP 6.3, §6.4): what a hit does once the rules have decided it, generic and without formulas,\n * stats or balance: `damage(w, target, hit)` takes `hit.amount` from the target's health, pushes its crowd body\n * away from the blow as momentum the solver carries on, throws it up, kills it into the corpse group with the kill\n * launch, asks for a hit-stop and emits the event `damage` for the view (numbers, sparks, sounds). How much a hit\n * takes and how hard it pushes are the game's numbers, passed in.\n *\n * - **Health**: the game keeps it in a component (`HEALTH_FIELDS`): hit points, and when and by whom it was last hit,\n *   which flinches, stuns and flashes read (`w.tick - health.hitAt`). At 0 or below it is dead.\n * - **Bodies**: a crowd agent's (engine/physics/crowd.ts): `knockback` adds the push the same for every mass and the\n *   solver carries it into the crowd; `launch` throws it up; `killAgent` moves it to the group `corpse`, which the\n *   crowd walks through, and `updateCrowd` removes it later.\n * - **Hit-stop**: a sim decision the frame clock carries out (engine/core/time.ts). `requestHitStop` keeps the longest\n *   stop asked in the step (they never add up); the run's host takes it after the step (`takeHitStop`) for\n *   `clock.hitStop`, whose leaky budget shrinks stops that follow closely, so a crowd hit stops time once however many\n *   it strikes. The clock is outside the hash: a stop changes how many steps a frame runs, never what a step does.\n *\n * Invariants: a dead or absent target (health at 0 or below, a body no longer `alive`), or an entity holding the\n * component `invulnerable` (god mode: engine/sim/devActions.ts), takes no hit: `damage` returns null and changes nothing. Everything a hit changes is in the target's components and its body, and the \n * plain data, so a capture restores it. The asked stop waits in a side table by world, an outbox the sim never reads\n * (no listener: a listener would join the world's captured schedule), so it never changes the hash. Only hosts take it:\n * engine/sim-api.ts leaves `takeHitStop` out. No clock, no random stream: a rule that wants randomness draws from a\n * named stream and passes the result in.\n *\n * Carried from `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:324-342` (`damageEnemy`: the knockback along the blow, the big\n * hit's launch, the kill launch and its 1.3 carry, the switch to the dead group, the freeze), the hit-stop through\n * the budget of `src/emberdeep/10-combat.js:45-76` (engine/core/time.ts).\n *\n * @example\n * import { createPhysics } from '../physics/world';\n * import { attachPhysics } from '../physics/binding';\n * import { createWorld } from './world';\n * const w = createWorld({ seed: 1 });\n * attachPhysics(w, createPhysics()); // a scene with physics: true has this\n * const health = { ...HEALTH, hp: 3 };\n * damage(w, { id: 4, health }, { amount: 5, from: [0, 0, 0], stop: 0.06 }); // { killed: true, hp: -2, … }; an agent's body is also knocked\n * takeHitStop(w); // 0.06: the host hands it to its frame clock (clock.hitStop), once\n * takeHitStop(w); // 0: taken\n * @see engine/sim/damage.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\nimport { copyValue, defineSchema, freezeValue, isPlainObject, show } from '../core/schema';\nimport { killAgent, knockback, launch, type CrowdAgent } from '../physics/crowd';\nimport type { Vec3Like } from '../physics/queries';\nimport { physicsOf } from '../physics/binding';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\n/** A target's health, for a scene's component: `defineComponent('health', { description, fields: HEALTH_FIELDS })`. */\nexport const HEALTH_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  hp: { type: 'number', default: 1, description: 'Hit points left: at 0 or below, it is dead.' },\n  hitAt: {\n    type: 'integer',\n    default: -1,\n    minimum: -1,\n    description: 'The step it was last hit (w.tick; −1: never): flinches, stuns and flashes count from it.',\n  },\n  hitBy: { type: 'integer', default: -1, minimum: -1, description: 'Who hit it last (an entity id; −1: unknown).' },\n});\n\n...\n/**\n * @file Use (PLAN.md WP 13.16; docs/PLAYGROUND.md §7.1): what a player's `interact` press uses, and what a strike does.\n * `addInteraction(w)` (a scene's setup, once) adds the system `interact` (phase `intents`, before the actors move and\n * before the doors, the panes and the switches, phase `anim`; the dev action `freeze` stops none of them). Each step\n * it finds, for every player actor, the usable thing in reach (`USE_REACH`, 2 m from its eye) and in view: along its\n * crosshair when the step has a `look` and an `aim` (first person and the shooter), the first body the eye's ray toward\n * the aim strikes, or a bodiless thing (a switch) it passes within `BODILESS` of before that; else the nearest within\n * 60° of where it faces, a wall, a door or a pane between hiding one. Only what can be used now counts (a sliding door\n * hides no switch). It keeps that thing and its prompt in the actor's `interactor` component, which the page shows with\n * the binding of the device used last.\n *\n * Usable things are asked of their entries, never their ids: `defineUsable(component, { prompt, use })` says how an\n * entity holding that component is used (a door: engine/sim/doors.ts; a switch: switches.ts), and a prop is usable when\n * its own `use` has a `prompt`, or when a player can carry it (dynamic, at most `CARRY_MASS`). Its `use` hook gets the\n * world, the entity and the user. A carried prop's body is driven 0.8 m in front of the eye each step (it still\n * collides, and drops when held back past the reach and 1.5 m); the next use throws it along the view at `THROW_SPEED`.\n * A strike (`strikeEntity`: a melee hit, engine/sim/melee.ts; a shot; a blast) lands on what takes one, the kind\n * `strikable` by component (`defineStrikable(component, strike, move?)`: a door bursts open, then breaks; a breakable\n * prop takes a point of damage; a thing a point), else a dynamic prop is knocked; a lamp or a switch takes none. The\n * entry may name the move a melee plays on it (a door's kick: `strikeEntry`).\n *\n * A use takes its press: `interactor.usedAt` is the step, and the actors' input leaves it out of the rides'\n * (engine/sim/actorInput.ts), so using a door beside a zipline does not ride it. Events: `use` `{ id, target }`,\n * `pickup` and `throw` (the same).\n *\n * Invariants: everything is in components (the interactor, the props', the doors'); candidates in id order, the\n * nearest first among them, ties to the lower id; the queries read the physics world as of the last step; a body's prop\n * is found by a scan of the props (`propOfBody`), never an index kept beside them.\n * @see engine/sim/interact.test.ts\n */\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { pressed, type Intents } from '../input/intents';\nimport { physicsOf } from '../physics/binding';\nimport { getProp, type Prop } from '../world/props/prop';\nimport type { ActorComponents } from './actors';\nimport { hitProp, propOfBody, type ScenePropComponents } from './props';\nimport { defineComponent } from './state';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\n/** How far from the eye a thing is in reach (m). */\nexport const USE_REACH = 2;\n/** The heaviest prop a player carries (kg): a chair, a box, a crate. */\nexport const CARRY_MASS = 25;\n/** How fast a carried prop leaves when it is thrown (m/s). */\nexport const THROW_SPEED = 8;\n/** The eye over the feet, standing and crouched (m): the cameras' (engine/gfx/cameras/effects.ts); a gun aims from it. */\nexport const EYE = { stand: 1.52, crouch: 0.81 } as const;\n/** How far in front of the eye a carried prop is held (m), and how much farther than the reach it may lag before it drops. */\nconst HOLD = { ahead: 0.8, slip: 1.5 } as const;\n/** The cosine of the widest angle from where a player faces that is \"in front\" (60°), written out: no Math at import. */\nconst FRONT = 0.5;\n/** How near a bodiless thing (a switch on a wall) the crosshair's ray must pass to be on it (m). */\nexport const BODILESS = 0.15;\n\n/** How an entity holding a component is used: what the HUD says (null: not now) and what using it does. */\nexport interface UsableSpec {\n  prompt(w: World, id: number, user: number): string | null;\n  use(w: World, id: number, user: number): void;\n...\n/**\n * @file Reactions and secondary motion on a scene's bodies (PLAN.md WP 6.2, WP 7.8, ADR-0018 amendment 1): the two\n * components that put engine/anim's reactions and secondary motion on an entity.\n *\n * - **`reactions`** (`REACTION_FIELDS`): a body's reactions (engine/anim/reactions.ts `Reactions`: the flinch spring,\n *   the knockdown, the death and the get-up chosen by how it lies) as the plain data `Reactions.save()` writes, so\n *   the hash, captures and replays hold them. `reactTo(w, id, act)` runs `act` on an entity's reactions, adding the\n *   component at rest the first time, and saves them back. `stepReactions(state, dt)` steps them one tick and returns\n *   what the phase playing tells the body (`down`, `pose`), to lay over its body states. The system `actors.anim`\n *   steps an actor's (engine/sim/actors.ts); game code steps any other body's in its own rig system (a crowd body's\n *   states, engine/sim/crowdBodies.ts). The view draws an actor's flinch and how it lies (engine/gfx/puppets/actors.ts).\n * - **`secondary`** (`SECONDARY_FIELDS`): the secondary motion the view hangs on an actor's puppet (engine/anim/\n *   secondary.ts `Secondary`: hair, a cape, a tail) and its colour. The sim only carries it, as it carries a build or\n *   a stand-in's material: the view steps the chains, so none of their motion reaches the hash.\n *\n * Invariants: everything a reaction remembers is in its component (the `Reactions` here are scratch, loaded at every\n * call), so a capture restores it exactly; a body at rest (no flinch, nothing playing) steps for nothing.\n *\n * @example\n * import { Vector3 } from '../core/math';\n * import { createWorld } from './world';\n * const w = createWorld({ seed: 1 });\n * const id = w.spawn({});\n * reactTo(w, id, (r) => r.knockDown(new Vector3(0, 0, -1), 0)); // struck from the front: it falls onto its back\n * stepReactions(w.get(id).reactions, w.dt); // { down: 1 }: what the body is told while it falls\n * @see engine/sim/reactions.test.ts\n */\nimport { REACTIONS, Reactions, type ReactionBody, type ReactionState } from '../anim/reactions';\nimport type { EventMap } from '../core/events';\nimport { codeError } from '../core/log';\nimport { registry as sharedRegistry, type Registry } from '../core/registry';\nimport { defineSchema } from '../core/schema';\nimport { SIM_DT } from '../core/time';\nimport { defineComponent } from './state';\nimport type { World } from './world';\n\nconst AT_REST: ReactionState = new Reactions().save();\n\n/** The component `reactions`' fields: `Reactions.save()`'s, at rest by default. */\nexport const REACTION_FIELDS = defineSchema({\n  flinch: {\n    type: 'array',\n    items: { type: 'number' },\n    default: AT_REST.flinch,\n    description: 'The flinch: its lean right and forward (tilts’ tangents), then their speeds per second.',","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AR2H4YE63SXzvgaPzLqLFT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
