{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FKG9b3xsNfUjHQNC9ABFPX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: the game\n2\t\n3\tThe game director's plan. You build from it without being able to ask me anything, so it settles every creative and\n4\ttechnical choice TASK.md leaves open. Build this game; don't re-decide it.\n5\t\n6\t## 0. How to use this plan\n7\t\n8\t- **TASK.md wins** on everything it states: your deliverable's file name, your dev folder's name, the date, the model and\n9\t  effort values, the exit interview's file name and shape. This plan never repeats those, because yours differ.\n10\t- **Words used here.** *Dev folder*: the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create; every path below is relative to it.\n11\t  *The deliverable*: the single HTML file TASK.md names, one folder above the dev folder. *The exit interview*: the JSON\n12\t  file TASK.md names, beside the deliverable.\n13\t- **[verified]** marks what I ran on this computer on 2026-11-04 with throwaway spikes, on engine commit `e7a1243`. If your\n14\t  clone is newer and something differs, trust the engine's error message: each one names its fix.\n15\t- **Read order.** §1 to §3 once, then §16 (setup, recipes, traps) before your first line of code, then follow §18's\n16\t  milestones and look up the rest as you reach it. §19 is the finish line.\n17\t\n18\t## 1. The game in one page\n19\t\n20\t**Pitch.** A 51-year-old insurance loss adjuster with a bad knee, a service pistol and an excellent tie is sent to a\n21\tfog-bound North Atlantic island where all 412 residents drowned, on paper, on the same night last March. They keep trying\n22\tto gut him. Shoot a knee, kick the crowd, sell the salvage, fit it all in the briefcase, keep the junior actuary alive,\n23\tand deny the claim.\n24\t\n25\t- **Genre:** third-person action horror with a light economy, plus a co-op survival mode.\n26\t- **Perspective:** over the shoulder (the engine's `shoulder` camera). Cinematic cameras for the title, cutscenes and\n27\t  attract mode only.\n28\t- **Length:** 40 to 60 minutes for a first campaign, 15 for someone who knows it, plus a replayable 5-minute co-op round.\n29\t- **Tone:** a straight-faced B-movie. Dread in the fog, a pun after the kill.\n30\t\n31\t**What makes it feel like its source, as mechanics (keep all five):**\n32\t\n33\t1. **Every bullet is a decision.** You aim with your feet planted. Where you hit matters: a knee drops a foe, a head\n34\t   reels it, and either one opens a kick that scatters the crowd.\n35\t2. **Pressure in the open.** Slow, smart-enough crowds that flank, throw and grab. You retreat, spend a bullet, kick,\n36\t   reposition. Never a corridor of pop-ups.\n37\t3. **The briefcase economy.** Grid inventory, a beloved shady merchant, salvage to sell, guns to service, adaptive drops.\n38\t4. **Something new every ten minutes.** Each zone has a set piece that changes the rules.\n39\t5. **Camp with conviction.** A hammy villain on the island's loudspeakers, a dry handler on the radio, a hero who quips.\n40\t\n41\t**Title.** Do what TASK.md says, first and silently: no file lists the 50 candidates. Put the winner in one constant,\n42\t`GAME_TITLE` in `labs/game/title.ts`, and everything reads it. Nothing in the story or dialogue mentions the title, so any\n43\ttitle fits.\n44\t\n45\t## 2. Originality rules\n46\t\n47\tTASK.md asks for an **original game inspired by the source**. Mechanics and genre carry over. Nothing else does.\n48\t\n49\t- No names, characters, lines, monsters, maps, music, logos or UI layouts from the source or its series. No secret-agent\n50\t  rescue, president's daughter, Spanish village, cloaked merchant catchphrases, church bell that ends a siege, chainsaw\n51\t  man in a sack, lake monster, mine-cart course, typewriter saves or coloured-herb names.\n52\t- Every set piece in §4 is its own. Build it as written here rather than as remembered from anything else.\n53\t- If a line could be quoted as the source's, rewrite it. Melodies are newly written, never quotations.\n54\t- The source is named only where TASK.md requires it (About/Credits, the exit interview, your reply).\n55\t- No real brands, firms or places.\n56\t\n57\t## 3. Story, cast, voice\n58\t\n59\t**Setting.** The island of **Sallow Skerry**, North Atlantic, sometime after the war and before anyone had a decent\n60\ttelephone. The sun has been almost setting since March. The village is **Orran's Rest**. The island's church is the\n61\t**Society of the Undertow**, which holds a life policy on every resident.\n62\t\n63\t**The hook.** Four hundred and twelve death claims, one night, one beneficiary. Your firm, **Pelham & Grice Mutual\n64\tAssurance**, wants it verified. The first adjuster, a junior actuary, stopped filing expenses two weeks ago, which is how\n65\tthey know something is wrong.\n66\t\n67\t**The truth.** A sea parasite, **the Lodger**, lives in the skull of everyone on the island. The thing it serves, **the\n68\tUndertow**, collects the souls; the policies launder the money to pay for more ships. Late twist: Pelham & Grice\n69\tcountersigned every policy. Head office is in on it.\n70\t\n71\t| Who | What they are | How they talk | Use |\n72\t|---|---|---|---|\n73\t| **Desmond Quill** (he/him) | Hero. 51, Senior Loss Adjuster, ex-marine, bad knee. Trench coat, red tie, briefcase. | Deadpan. Treats horror as paperwork. | Player |\n74\t| **Wren Adeyemi** (she/her) | 24, junior actuary. Rescued mid-game. Quick, unimpressed. | Speaks in odds. | Escort partner |\n75\t| **Mrs. Thripp** (she/her) | Head of Claims, on the pocket radio. | Passive-aggressive memos. | Handler, then betrayer |\n76\t| **Prior Ambrose Goole** (he/him) | Cult leader, on the island's Tannoy loudspeakers. | Full ham. Loves the word \"premium\". | Final boss |\n77\t| **Harbourmaster Tolliver Brack** (he/him) | 5 m tall oilskin brute, an anglerfish lure growing from his chest, an anchor on a chain. | Few words, all threats. | Boss |\n78\t| **Mr. Lionel Sedge** (he/him) | Fraud Investigation. Quill's old partner, turned. Thin, polite, cane sword. | Silky insults. | Duel |\n79\t| **The Salvor** (he/him) | Merchant. Oilskin hood, dripping wet, rowboat, lantern, big grin. Always there before you. | Jolly, never explains. | Shop, comic relief |\n80\t\n81\t**Voice rules.** All speech is gibberish audio (§13) with subtitles. Subtitles are always on, big and name-tagged. Write\n82\tthe rest of the script in exactly this style. Keep lines short: a TV viewer reads them in two seconds.\n83\t\n84\t```text\n85\tQUILL  Mr. Harrow? Pelham and Grice. I'm here about your death.\n86\tQUILL  (after the clerk attacks) I'll put that down as \"contested\".\n87\tTHRIPP Mr. Quill, I remind you that ammunition is not an allowable expense.\n88\tGOOLE  (Tannoy) Mr. Quill! You arrive at last, clutching your little forms. Do you know what the sea charges for a soul?\n89\tGOOLE  (Tannoy) Everything. And it pays in full.\n90\tQUILL  Funny. That's what my ex-wife's lawyer said.\n91\tTHRIPP Did you get a signature?\n92\tQUILL  (first Lodger bursts from a neck) Is that covered?\n93\tTHRIPP Pre-existing condition.\n94\tWREN   You're late.\n95\tQUILL  There was a queue.\n96\tWREN   Seventy-three percent chance that door is a bad idea.\n97\tQUILL  And the other twenty-seven?\n98\tWREN   Worse door.\n99\tSALVOR Come in out the wet, friend! Not that it makes a difference.\n100\tSALVOR Found it, fixed it, flogged it.\n101\tSALVOR That'll be dear.\n102\tSALVOR Mind the tide.\n103\tSEDGE  Still doing it by the book, Desmond? I ate the book.\n104\tBRACK  Harbour dues.\n105\tGOOLE  You cannot insure against the tide!\n106\tQUILL  No. But I can cancel your policy.\n107\tTHRIPP Head office regrets to inform you that your claim has been denied.\n108\tQUILL  Consider this my notice.\n109\t```\n110\t\n111\tKill quips (rare, on a 25 s cooldown, never during a subtitle): \"Policy void.\" \"You're not covered.\" \"Read the small\n112\tprint.\" \"That's what we call a total loss.\" \"Your premium's gone up.\" \"Non-refundable.\" Quill straightens his tie after a\n113\tbig fight: that is his signature gesture (§12).\n114\t\n115\tGame over is a rubber stamp, **CLAIM DENIED**, with a cause of loss chosen by what killed you (\"Cause of loss: gaff.\",\n116\t\"Cause of loss: anchor.\", \"Cause of loss: beneficiary mislaid.\").\n117\t\n118\t## 4. The world and the campaign\n119\t\n120\t**One connected island, one scene.** A running page cannot switch scenes (`APP_OTHER_SCENE`) [verified], so the campaign\n121\tis a single site, `site:skerry`, about 360 × 360 m, with every zone standing in it and gates between them. A 320 m site\n122\twith 41 buildings reached `ready` in 26 s on the software renderer [verified], which a real GPU beats easily. Teleports,\n123\tdev-mode jumps, saves and attract mode all use the same world with no reload.\n124\t\n125\t```text\n126\t                         N: cliffs and open sea\n127\t   Lighthouse (110,20) -- Causeway -- Abbey of St. Orran (250,40) -- Funicular top (300,40)\n128\t                                                                       |  cliff\n129\t       Marsh chapel (55,130)        Brack's Cannery (150,110) -- Funicular foot (230,110)\n130\t             |                             |\n131\t       Marsh path (70,170) ----------------+\n132\t             |\n133\t       The Shingle (90,262) ---- Orran's Rest Square (180,240) [the beached trawler]\n134\t                                           |\n135\t                                     Fish Lane (180,285)\n136\t                                     Harbour Office (180,312)\n137\t                                     The Slip (180,340)\n138\t                         S: open sea\n139\t```\n140\t\n141\tCoordinates are x east, z south, in metres, and are guides. Paths are 6 to 10 m wide, walled by low walls, nets, boats and\n142\trock, with lockable gates so the story stays linear while the island feels whole. Stand the terrain 3 m under the sea\n143\tplane and raise the land with `hills`, so the shoreline comes from the terrain; `flats` level each zone.\n144\t\n145\t**The beat sheet.** Beats are data (§16.2). Each has a zone, a trigger, what it spawns and says, and an exit condition.\n146\t\n147\t| Beat | Zone | What happens | Teaches |\n148\t|---|---|---|---|\n149\t| Cold open | Slip | Rowboat cutscene in fog. Stamp card: *Claim No. 00417*. Thripp's briefing. | Radio, subtitles |\n150\t| The Slip | Slip | Walk the pier. Gulls, wind, a distant foghorn. | Move, look, run, use |\n151\t| Harbour Office | Office (2 storeys) | Clerk Harrow at his desk: \"here about your death\". He attacks. Then two more. Filing cabinet, rum, ammo. | Aim, fire, knee, kick, reload, save, Case, pick up |\n152\t| Fish Lane | Lane | Three Parishioners among drying racks. Breakable crates, first salvage. Opens on the Square. | Crowd spacing, breakables, loot |\n153\t| **The Mary Constance** | Square | §4.1, the first set piece: a siege on a beached trawler. | Everything under pressure |\n154\t| Foghorn | Square | The horn sounds. Every foe stops, drops its tool, turns and walks into the sea. Silence. Quill: \"I'll need that in writing.\" Harbour key. | Dread |\n155\t| The Salvor | Shingle | First shop. Claims Practice shooting gallery. A seal in a net (free it, or don't). Glass floats start. | Buy, sell, service |\n156\t| Marsh path | Marsh | Thick fog on plank walks. Bog Hounds, gin traps, tripwire charges, Netwives on stilts throwing hatchets. Flare pistol on a corpse. | Shoot projectiles and traps |\n157\t| Marsh chapel | Chapel | First head-shot burst: a Lodger whips out of the neck (\"Is that covered?\"). A Deacon chanting. Ledger page. Cabinet. | Lodgers, flares, priority targets |\n158\t| Cannery yard | Cannery | Outdoor brawl among stacked barrels of fish oil. Second Gutter. | Fire, hazards |\n159\t| Cold Store | Cannery | Wren in a locker: \"You're late.\" Escort begins. | Wait, follow, hide |\n160\t| The Line Floor | Cannery | Conveyors and rail hooks. Foes carry Wren toward the tide chutes. Hatchmen with shields. Boost up a ledge. | Rescue, shields, flanking |\n161\t| **The Hoist** | Cannery | §4.2: Wren cranks for 60 s while you hold the gantry. | Defending a partner |\n162\t| **The Gutting Floor** | Cannery | §7.3: Harbourmaster Brack. The freed seal comes back. Shotgun or rifle reward. | Boss weak points |\n163\t| Loading bay | Cannery | Salvor again. Thripp sounds wrong on the radio. | Breather |\n164\t| **The Funicular** | Cliff | §4.3: a moving-platform shootout, then a jump prompt when the cable snaps. | Prompts, moving aim |\n165\t| Cloister | Abbey | Deacons, Hatchmen and Whips by lantern light. Cabinet. | Mixed crowds |\n166\t| **Records Vault** | Abbey | Ledger dial puzzle with Wren. The reveal: Pelham & Grice countersigned everything. | Puzzle, story turn |\n167\t| **The Audit** | Abbey | §7.3: the duel with Mr. Sedge. | Parry mastery |\n168\t| Notice given | Abbey | Thripp's betrayal call. Salvor on the steps. | Story |\n169\t| **The Causeway** | Causeway | §7.3: tide rising, Prior Goole, then the Undertow. Lighthouse beam, Whaling Gun. | Finale |\n170\t| Paid in full | Sea | Rowboat out at last light. \"Mark it paid in full.\" Results, rank, *Thank you for choosing Pelham & Grice.* | |\n171\t\n172\t**Collectibles.** 16 pieces of salvage (some take gems, worth more combined), 12 glass net floats to shoot (all 12 earn a\n173\tSalvor reward), 6 ledger pages (3 are required), 5 Thripp memos (jokes). A purchasable Chart pins salvage on the map.\n174\t\n175\t### 4.1 The Mary Constance: the trawler siege\n176\t\n177\tThe game's signature scene. A wrecked trawler has been hauled up a slipway into the middle of the Square as a shrine, with\n178\tcandles along the rails and a bonfire at her bow. Eight cottages ring the Square; two can be entered, with loot upstairs.\n179\t\n180\t- **The ship.** Hull about 24 × 8 m. Deck at 2.4 m with a railing, a wheelhouse at the stern (door, windows, a ladder to its\n181\t  roof at 4.8 m), a hold hatch. Collision comes from a level; the hull's curved look comes from props.\n182\t- **Boarding points.** Four gangplanks, two a side, plus a stern ladder. Foes path to a plank's foot, walk up it, and hop the\n183\t  rail. This is scripted movement between the ground's nav and the deck's, so no nav over ramps is needed.\n184\t- **Kick the plank.** Prompt at a plank's top: it falls, with anyone on it. Foes re-lay it after 25 s. This is the\n185\t  scene's main tool, and it turns a siege into a rhythm.\n186\t- **Winch nets.** A cargo net of crates hangs over each side. Shoot the cable or pull the lever: 6 m crush, one use each.\n187\t- **Over the side.** Vault the rail anywhere. Running to a cottage for ammo with the crowd behind you is the scene's best\n188\t  moment. Oil drums on the slipway burn.\n189\t- **Flow.** Arrival: Quill walks in during the sermon, Parishioners kneeling round the fire with their backs to him,\n190\t  Goole on the Tannoy. Then they turn. 14 on the field at most, spawning from six alleys out of sight. Netwives from\n191\t  minute 1. The Gutter at minute 2, heralded by his saw engine from far off. **No visible timer.** The horn sounds at\n192\t  3:00 or at 40 kills, whichever is first. Killing the Gutter is optional and pays well.\n193\t\n194\t### 4.2 The Hoist\n195\t\n196\tThe gantry over the Line Floor. Wren cranks a fish hoist for 60 s (a progress bar on her). Foes come up two ladders and over\n197\ta conveyor. Anyone who reaches her stops the crank until you hit them. Rail hooks can be shot loose to swing through a\n198\tlane. Wren calls flanks with odds: \"Left ladder. Eighty percent.\"\n199\t\n200\t### 4.3 The Funicular\n201\t\n202\tA fish-hoist car on a cliff cable, 90 s from foot to top, with a `driveMover` kinematic platform carrying riders. You stand\n203\ton the deck and aim freely as Parishioners leap from gantries and Netwives throw from ledges; Wren works the brake. At the\n204\ttop the cable snaps: a large jump prompt, then a scramble up a ladder with the car falling away behind you. Failing the\n205\tprompt costs a checkpoint, not much else.\n206\t\n207\t### 4.4 Small things\n208\t\n209\t- **Claims Practice** (Shingle): a coin-op gallery on a rotten pier, the Salvor's pride. 60 s, cardboard claimants on tracks\n210\t  (`defineThing`, `spawnThing`), hit the frauds, spare the widows. Score pays shillings, and beating it gives a free\n211\t  Firepower level. Enter and leave through a prompt, in the same world.\n212\t- **Glass floats:** 12 blue glass net floats hung about the island. They shatter when shot (`addBreakables`). All 12: the Salvor\n213\t  gives you a rare gem worth 5,000. Progress shows in the Claim File.\n214\t- **The ledger dials** (Vault): three big number wheels, one on screen at a time. The numbers are in the 3 required ledger pages,\n215\t  which Wren reads aloud on request. Left and right pick a wheel, up and down turn it, mouse clicks and wheel too.\n216\t- **The seal:** a grey blob-rig seal in a net on the Shingle. Shoot the net rope to free it, and it barks and flops off. Payoff in §7.3.\n217\t\n218\t## 5. Player verbs and controls\n219\t\n220\tQuill walks and runs, never jumps. Vaults, ladders, kicks and doors are context actions on one button.\n221\t\n222\t| Action | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Engine action |\n223\t|---|---|---|---|\n224\t| Move | Left stick | W A S D | `forward back left right` |\n225\t| Look | Right stick | Mouse (pointer lock) | |\n226\t| Aim, hold (feet plant) | L2 (LT) | Right mouse | `aim` |\n227\t| Fire, only while aiming | R2 (RT) | Left mouse | `fire` |\n228\t| Use, kick, vault, climb, open | Cross (A) | E | `interact` |\n229\t| Letter opener: slash, or parry if timed | R1 (RB) | F | `melee` |\n230\t| Run, hold | Circle (B) | Left Shift | `sprint` |\n231\t| Reload | Square (X) | R | `reload` |\n232\t| Case | Triangle (Y) | Tab | `case` (view action) |\n233\t| Wren: tap stay or follow, hold to hide | L1 (LB) | G | `partner` |\n234\t| Quick turn 180° | R3 | C | `quickturn` |\n235\t| Swap shoulder | L3 | V | `shoulder` (view action) |\n236\t| Next and previous weapon | D-pad right and left | Wheel, or 1 to 5 directly | `weaponNext weaponPrev weapon1…5` |\n237\t| Ready a throwable (aim shows the arc, fire throws) | D-pad up | Q | `throwable` |\n238\t| Drink the best heal | D-pad down | H | `heal` |\n239\t| Claim File (map, objectives) | Create (View) | M | `map` (view action) |\n240\t| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc | system `pause` |\n241\t\n242\tEngine codes: Cross `PadSouth`, Circle `PadEast`, Square `PadWest`, Triangle `PadNorth`, L1 `PadLeftShoulder`, R1 `PadRightShoulder`, L2\n243\t`PadLeftTrigger`, R2 `PadRightTrigger`, L3 `PadLeftStick`, R3 `PadRightStick`, D-pad `PadUp PadDown PadLeft PadRight`, Create `PadSelect`,\n244\tOptions `PadStart` (the system `pause`), sticks `PadLeftStickUp` and so on, wheel `WheelUp` `WheelDown`, mouse `MouseLeft` `MouseRight`.\n245\t\n246\tWith keyboard and mouse, when not aiming, a left click on a thing whose prompt is showing uses it. Everything rebinds on\n247\tthe Controls screen (`input.bindings.rebind`, `input.listen`, kept by `createEngine({ store })`). In a hub session the game\n248\tignores Options while Create is held, since that chord is the hub's.\n249\t\n250\t**Feel targets.** Run 3.4 m/s, sprint 5.2 m/s. Aim raise 0.22 s. Planted while aiming, turning with the view. Shot response\n251\ton the same step. Quick turn in 0.25 s (feed it through `cameraInput`). Camera arm 2.8 m, pulling in to 1.5 m while aiming.\n252\t\n253\t**Laser sight.** The red laser is the game's cursor: there is no crosshair. A line from the muzzle to a glowing dot where\n254\tthe shot will go, with a small wobble (≤ 0.3°, less with Handling upgrades, added to the camera's look so it affects `aim`).\n255\t§16.4 says how to draw it.\n256\t\n257\t**Aim assist (pad only, Settings, on by default).** While the centre ray is on a foe, stick look slows by 45%, plus a pull of at\n258\tmost 2°/s toward the foe's chest. Never for the mouse.\n259\t\n260\t## 6. Combat\n261\t\n262\t### 6.1 Hit zones and reactions\n263\t\n264\tA shot is a ray. Work out the zone from where the ray met the capsule, relative to the actor's feet, shifted by its state\n265\t(a kneeling foe's head is lower). Scale by build for big bodies. A zone-from-height check works [verified: head, torso and\n266\tlegs all came back correct].\n267\t\n268\t| Zone (height ÷ standing height) | Damage | Reaction |\n269\t|---|---|---|\n270\t| Head (above 0.80) | ×2.4 | **Reel**, 0.9 s: clutches its face. Kick window. |\n271\t| Torso (0.33 to 0.80) | ×1 | Flinch, 0.25 s. A thrower's throw is cancelled. |\n272\t| Arm (torso height, far from the axis) | ×0.6 | Drops its weapon: it picks it up (2 s) or claws. |\n273\t| Legs (below 0.33) | ×0.7 | **Kneel**, 1.2 s. Kick window. |\n274\t\n275\t**Kick (Cross/E on a reeling or kneeling foe within 2.2 m).** Roundhouse, 70 damage in a 2 m radius. Everyone in range goes\n276\tdown as a ragdoll, gets up after about 2 s. Hero is invulnerable for 0.6 s; 80 ms hit-stop and a camera shake. This is the\n277\tgame's central pleasure: guard it and make it land hard.\n278\t\n279\t**Slash (R1/F).** 18 damage, 0.45 s, breaks crates, hits downed foes for ×2. **Parry:** a press in the 0.2 s before a melee\n280\thit lands negates it and reels the attacker. Sparks and a clang; 120 ms hit-stop.\n281\t\n282\t**Grabbed.** A foe can grab you (10 damage, then 4 a second): waggle or mash to break out (§6.3). Other foes get free hits.\n283\t\n284\t**Lodger burst.** A foe killed by a head shot may burst into a Lodger Whip. The chance is `game.burst`, set per zone: 0 in the\n285\tvillage, 15% in the Marsh, 30% in the Cannery, 45% at the Abbey. You never know, so head shots stay a gamble.\n286\t\n287\t### 6.2 Weapons\n288\t\n289\tFired by a **game-owned `game.guns` system** on the engine's `weapons` component, not the engine's `addWeapons` (§16.3 says why).\n290\t\n291\t| Weapon (flavour name) | Case cells | Mag | Shots/s | Damage | Reload | Notes | Price |\n292\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n293\t| Company Issue pistol, \"The Deductible\" | 3×2 | 10 | 3.2 | 28 | 1.4 s | Start weapon | free |\n294\t| Pump gun, \"Full Coverage\" | 6×2 | 6 | 1.0 | 8 × 11 | 0.55 s a shell | 5° cone, pushes groups back | 1,800 |\n295\t| Bolt rifle, \"The Long Policy\" | 8×2 | 5 | 0.9 | 85 | 2.6 s | Scope (hold aim + D-pad up to zoom), pierces 2 | 3,500 |\n296\t| Trench gun, \"Premium Rate\" | 4×2 | 30 | 11 | 12 | 2.2 s | Automatic, climbing recoil | 6,000 |\n297\t| Flare pistol | 3×2 | 1 | 1.0 | 15 + burn 40 over 4 s | 1.6 s | Kills a Lodger Whip outright, lights oil | found |\n298\t| Letter opener | n/a | | | 18 | | Always on hand | |\n299\t| Whaling Gun | story | 1 | | 5,000 | | Finale only (§7.3) | 1 shilling |\n300\t\n301\tThrowables, each with an arc preview: **Oil Bomb** (1×2, a fire pool for 5 s), **Magnesium Flash** (1×1, stuns within 8 m for\n302\t3 s, kills exposed Lodgers), **Limpet Charge** (1×2, frag). Foes' thrown hatchets can be shot out of the air.\n303\t\n304\tDraw guns bigger than life (about 1.3×) so they read at 10 feet.\n305\t\n306\t### 6.3 Prompts under pressure\n307\t\n308\tOne small `qte` system: a prompt has a glyph, a window, and a result on success and failure. Three types: **press** (one button\n309\tin a window), **mash** (fill a meter), **parry** (any `melee` in a window). Drawn huge at screen centre with a shrinking\n310\tring. Used for the funicular jump, breaking grabs, Sedge's lunges, the finale. A dev slider scales every window.\n311\t\n312\t### 6.4 The Risk Rating (hidden dynamic difficulty)\n313\t\n314\tA hidden 0 to 10 score, `game.risk`, in the sim. Accuracy, kills without damage and speed push it up; deaths and being\n315\thit push it down. It scales foe aggression, attacker tokens and damage by ±15%. Settings' difficulty (Lenient, Standard,\n316\tPunitive) sets its range and base numbers. The results screen reveals it as a joke: \"Risk rating: uninsurable\".\n317\t\n318\t## 7. Enemies and bosses\n319\t\n320\tHumanoids are **actors**, not crowd agents: 80 animated actors with clips cost about 5 ms a step [verified], and actors\n321\tget the rich reactions, clips and ragdolls that make hits read. Hounds and Leeches use the engine's crowd rigs.\n322\t\n323\t### 7.1 The roster\n324\t\n325\t| Foe | HP | m/s | Attack | Special | First seen |\n326\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n327\t| **Parishioner** (oilskins, knit jumper; gaff, oar, gutting knife) | 100 | 1.25 to 1.6 | Swing 18, wind-up 0.7 s; grab | The bread and butter. Mutters scripture. | Office |\n328\t| **Netwife** (apron, headscarf) | 80 | 1.2 | Hatchet 15, arcing at 12 m/s | Keeps 8 to 14 m. Hatchets can be shot down. | Square |\n329\t| **The Gutter** (rubber apron, a lobster pot over his head, a motorised flensing saw on a pole) | 900 | 1.9 | Sweep 60; a grab kills below 35 HP | Engine noise from far off. Stagger only by head or knee. Pays 2,500. | Square |\n330\t| **Bog Hound** (quadruped rig) | 60 | 5.5 | Leap bite 12 | Packs of 3 to 5. One shot staggers. | Marsh |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed5deb30-576e-4aa7-93e0-da5eaa4ce63e","uuid":"c0c72d19-0773-4101-a08a-a9672a685506","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:20:37.852Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-212002__x601dca/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: the game\n\nThe game director's plan. You build from it without being able to ask me anything, so it settles every creative and\ntechnical choice TASK.md leaves open. Build this game; don't re-decide it.\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **TASK.md wins** on everything it states: your deliverable's file name, your dev folder's name, the date, the model and\n  effort values, the exit interview's file name and shape. This plan never repeats those, because yours differ.\n- **Words used here.** *Dev folder*: the subfolder TASK.md tells you to create; every path below is relative to it.\n  *The deliverable*: the single HTML file TASK.md names, one folder above the dev folder. *The exit interview*: the JSON\n  file TASK.md names, beside the deliverable.\n- **[verified]** marks what I ran on this computer on 2026-11-04 with throwaway spikes, on engine commit `e7a1243`. If your\n  clone is newer and something differs, trust the engine's error message: each one names its fix.\n- **Read order.** §1 to §3 once, then §16 (setup, recipes, traps) before your first line of code, then follow §18's\n  milestones and look up the rest as you reach it. §19 is the finish line.\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n**Pitch.** A 51-year-old insurance loss adjuster with a bad knee, a service pistol and an excellent tie is sent to a\nfog-bound North Atlantic island where all 412 residents drowned, on paper, on the same night last March. They keep trying\nto gut him. Shoot a knee, kick the crowd, sell the salvage, fit it all in the briefcase, keep the junior actuary alive,\nand deny the claim.\n\n- **Genre:** third-person action horror with a light economy, plus a co-op survival mode.\n- **Perspective:** over the shoulder (the engine's `shoulder` camera). Cinematic cameras for the title, cutscenes and\n  attract mode only.\n- **Length:** 40 to 60 minutes for a first campaign, 15 for someone who knows it, plus a replayable 5-minute co-op round.\n- **Tone:** a straight-faced B-movie. Dread in the fog, a pun after the kill.\n\n**What makes it feel like its source, as mechanics (keep all five):**\n\n1. **Every bullet is a decision.** You aim with your feet planted. Where you hit matters: a knee drops a foe, a head\n   reels it, and either one opens a kick that scatters the crowd.\n2. **Pressure in the open.** Slow, smart-enough crowds that flank, throw and grab. You retreat, spend a bullet, kick,\n   reposition. Never a corridor of pop-ups.\n3. **The briefcase economy.** Grid inventory, a beloved shady merchant, salvage to sell, guns to service, adaptive drops.\n4. **Something new every ten minutes.** Each zone has a set piece that changes the rules.\n5. **Camp with conviction.** A hammy villain on the island's loudspeakers, a dry handler on the radio, a hero who quips.\n\n**Title.** Do what TASK.md says, first and silently: no file lists the 50 candidates. Put the winner in one constant,\n`GAME_TITLE` in `labs/game/title.ts`, and everything reads it. Nothing in the story or dialogue mentions the title, so any\ntitle fits.\n\n## 2. Originality rules\n\nTASK.md asks for an **original game inspired by the source**. Mechanics and genre carry over. Nothing else does.\n\n- No names, characters, lines, monsters, maps, music, logos or UI layouts from the source or its series. No secret-agent\n  rescue, president's daughter, Spanish village, cloaked merchant catchphrases, church bell that ends a siege, chainsaw\n  man in a sack, lake monster, mine-cart course, typewriter saves or coloured-herb names.\n- Every set piece in §4 is its own. Build it as written here rather than as remembered from anything else.\n- If a line could be quoted as the source's, rewrite it. Melodies are newly written, never quotations.\n- The source is named only where TASK.md requires it (About/Credits, the exit interview, your reply).\n- No real brands, firms or places.\n\n## 3. Story, cast, voice\n\n**Setting.** The island of **Sallow Skerry**, North Atlantic, sometime after the war and before anyone had a decent\ntelephone. The sun has been almost setting since March. The village is **Orran's Rest**. The island's church is the\n**Society of the Undertow**, which holds a life policy on every resident.\n\n**The hook.** Four hundred and twelve death claims, one night, one beneficiary. Your firm, **Pelham & Grice Mutual\nAssurance**, wants it verified. The first adjuster, a junior actuary, stopped filing expenses two weeks ago, which is how\nthey know something is wrong.\n\n**The truth.** A sea parasite, **the Lodger**, lives in the skull of everyone on the island. The thing it serves, **the\nUndertow**, collects the souls; the policies launder the money to pay for more ships. Late twist: Pelham & Grice\ncountersigned every policy. Head office is in on it.\n\n| Who | What they are | How they talk | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Desmond Quill** (he/him) | Hero. 51, Senior Loss Adjuster, ex-marine, bad knee. Trench coat, red tie, briefcase. | Deadpan. Treats horror as paperwork. | Player |\n| **Wren Adeyemi** (she/her) | 24, junior actuary. Rescued mid-game. Quick, unimpressed. | Speaks in odds. | Escort partner |\n| **Mrs. Thripp** (she/her) | Head of Claims, on the pocket radio. | Passive-aggressive memos. | Handler, then betrayer |\n| **Prior Ambrose Goole** (he/him) | Cult leader, on the island's Tannoy loudspeakers. | Full ham. Loves the word \"premium\". | Final boss |\n| **Harbourmaster Tolliver Brack** (he/him) | 5 m tall oilskin brute, an anglerfish lure growing from his chest, an anchor on a chain. | Few words, all threats. | Boss |\n| **Mr. Lionel Sedge** (he/him) | Fraud Investigation. Quill's old partner, turned. Thin, polite, cane sword. | Silky insults. | Duel |\n| **The Salvor** (he/him) | Merchant. Oilskin hood, dripping wet, rowboat, lantern, big grin. Always there before you. | Jolly, never explains. | Shop, comic relief |\n\n**Voice rules.** All speech is gibberish audio (§13) with subtitles. Subtitles are always on, big and name-tagged. Write\nthe rest of the script in exactly this style. Keep lines short: a TV viewer reads them in two seconds.\n\n```text\nQUILL  Mr. Harrow? Pelham and Grice. I'm here about your death.\nQUILL  (after the clerk attacks) I'll put that down as \"contested\".\nTHRIPP Mr. Quill, I remind you that ammunition is not an allowable expense.\nGOOLE  (Tannoy) Mr. Quill! You arrive at last, clutching your little forms. Do you know what the sea charges for a soul?\nGOOLE  (Tannoy) Everything. And it pays in full.\nQUILL  Funny. That's what my ex-wife's lawyer said.\nTHRIPP Did you get a signature?\nQUILL  (first Lodger bursts from a neck) Is that covered?\nTHRIPP Pre-existing condition.\nWREN   You're late.\nQUILL  There was a queue.\nWREN   Seventy-three percent chance that door is a bad idea.\nQUILL  And the other twenty-seven?\nWREN   Worse door.\nSALVOR Come in out the wet, friend! Not that it makes a difference.\nSALVOR Found it, fixed it, flogged it.\nSALVOR That'll be dear.\nSALVOR Mind the tide.\nSEDGE  Still doing it by the book, Desmond? I ate the book.\nBRACK  Harbour dues.\nGOOLE  You cannot insure against the tide!\nQUILL  No. But I can cancel your policy.\nTHRIPP Head office regrets to inform you that your claim has been denied.\nQUILL  Consider this my notice.\n```\n\nKill quips (rare, on a 25 s cooldown, never during a subtitle): \"Policy void.\" \"You're not covered.\" \"Read the small\nprint.\" \"That's what we call a total loss.\" \"Your premium's gone up.\" \"Non-refundable.\" Quill straightens his tie after a\nbig fight: that is his signature gesture (§12).\n\nGame over is a rubber stamp, **CLAIM DENIED**, with a cause of loss chosen by what killed you (\"Cause of loss: gaff.\",\n\"Cause of loss: anchor.\", \"Cause of loss: beneficiary mislaid.\").\n\n## 4. The world and the campaign\n\n**One connected island, one scene.** A running page cannot switch scenes (`APP_OTHER_SCENE`) [verified], so the campaign\nis a single site, `site:skerry`, about 360 × 360 m, with every zone standing in it and gates between them. A 320 m site\nwith 41 buildings reached `ready` in 26 s on the software renderer [verified], which a real GPU beats easily. Teleports,\ndev-mode jumps, saves and attract mode all use the same world with no reload.\n\n```text\n                         N: cliffs and open sea\n   Lighthouse (110,20) -- Causeway -- Abbey of St. Orran (250,40) -- Funicular top (300,40)\n                                                                       |  cliff\n       Marsh chapel (55,130)        Brack's Cannery (150,110) -- Funicular foot (230,110)\n             |                             |\n       Marsh path (70,170) ----------------+\n             |\n       The Shingle (90,262) ---- Orran's Rest Square (180,240) [the beached trawler]\n                                           |\n                                     Fish Lane (180,285)\n                                     Harbour Office (180,312)\n                                     The Slip (180,340)\n                         S: open sea\n```\n\nCoordinates are x east, z south, in metres, and are guides. Paths are 6 to 10 m wide, walled by low walls, nets, boats and\nrock, with lockable gates so the story stays linear while the island feels whole. Stand the terrain 3 m under the sea\nplane and raise the land with `hills`, so the shoreline comes from the terrain; `flats` level each zone.\n\n**The beat sheet.** Beats are data (§16.2). Each has a zone, a trigger, what it spawns and says, and an exit condition.\n\n| Beat | Zone | What happens | Teaches |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cold open | Slip | Rowboat cutscene in fog. Stamp card: *Claim No. 00417*. Thripp's briefing. | Radio, subtitles |\n| The Slip | Slip | Walk the pier. Gulls, wind, a distant foghorn. | Move, look, run, use |\n| Harbour Office | Office (2 storeys) | Clerk Harrow at his desk: \"here about your death\". He attacks. Then two more. Filing cabinet, rum, ammo. | Aim, fire, knee, kick, reload, save, Case, pick up |\n| Fish Lane | Lane | Three Parishioners among drying racks. Breakable crates, first salvage. Opens on the Square. | Crowd spacing, breakables, loot |\n| **The Mary Constance** | Square | §4.1, the first set piece: a siege on a beached trawler. | Everything under pressure |\n| Foghorn | Square | The horn sounds. Every foe stops, drops its tool, turns and walks into the sea. Silence. Quill: \"I'll need that in writing.\" Harbour key. | Dread |\n| The Salvor | Shingle | First shop. Claims Practice shooting gallery. A seal in a net (free it, or don't). Glass floats start. | Buy, sell, service |\n| Marsh path | Marsh | Thick fog on plank walks. Bog Hounds, gin traps, tripwire charges, Netwives on stilts throwing hatchets. Flare pistol on a corpse. | Shoot projectiles and traps |\n| Marsh chapel | Chapel | First head-shot burst: a Lodger whips out of the neck (\"Is that covered?\"). A Deacon chanting. Ledger page. Cabinet. | Lodgers, flares, priority targets |\n| Cannery yard | Cannery | Outdoor brawl among stacked barrels of fish oil. Second Gutter. | Fire, hazards |\n| Cold Store | Cannery | Wren in a locker: \"You're late.\" Escort begins. | Wait, follow, hide |\n| The Line Floor | Cannery | Conveyors and rail hooks. Foes carry Wren toward the tide chutes. Hatchmen with shields. Boost up a ledge. | Rescue, shields, flanking |\n| **The Hoist** | Cannery | §4.2: Wren cranks for 60 s while you hold the gantry. | Defending a partner |\n| **The Gutting Floor** | Cannery | §7.3: Harbourmaster Brack. The freed seal comes back. Shotgun or rifle reward. | Boss weak points |\n| Loading bay | Cannery | Salvor again. Thripp sounds wrong on the radio. | Breather |\n| **The Funicular** | Cliff | §4.3: a moving-platform shootout, then a jump prompt when the cable snaps. | Prompts, moving aim |\n| Cloister | Abbey | Deacons, Hatchmen and Whips by lantern light. Cabinet. | Mixed crowds |\n| **Records Vault** | Abbey | Ledger dial puzzle with Wren. The reveal: Pelham & Grice countersigned everything. | Puzzle, story turn |\n| **The Audit** | Abbey | §7.3: the duel with Mr. Sedge. | Parry mastery |\n| Notice given | Abbey | Thripp's betrayal call. Salvor on the steps. | Story |\n| **The Causeway** | Causeway | §7.3: tide rising, Prior Goole, then the Undertow. Lighthouse beam, Whaling Gun. | Finale |\n| Paid in full | Sea | Rowboat out at last light. \"Mark it paid in full.\" Results, rank, *Thank you for choosing Pelham & Grice.* | |\n\n**Collectibles.** 16 pieces of salvage (some take gems, worth more combined), 12 glass net floats to shoot (all 12 earn a\nSalvor reward), 6 ledger pages (3 are required), 5 Thripp memos (jokes). A purchasable Chart pins salvage on the map.\n\n### 4.1 The Mary Constance: the trawler siege\n\nThe game's signature scene. A wrecked trawler has been hauled up a slipway into the middle of the Square as a shrine, with\ncandles along the rails and a bonfire at her bow. Eight cottages ring the Square; two can be entered, with loot upstairs.\n\n- **The ship.** Hull about 24 × 8 m. Deck at 2.4 m with a railing, a wheelhouse at the stern (door, windows, a ladder to its\n  roof at 4.8 m), a hold hatch. Collision comes from a level; the hull's curved look comes from props.\n- **Boarding points.** Four gangplanks, two a side, plus a stern ladder. Foes path to a plank's foot, walk up it, and hop the\n  rail. This is scripted movement between the ground's nav and the deck's, so no nav over ramps is needed.\n- **Kick the plank.** Prompt at a plank's top: it falls, with anyone on it. Foes re-lay it after 25 s. This is the\n  scene's main tool, and it turns a siege into a rhythm.\n- **Winch nets.** A cargo net of crates hangs over each side. Shoot the cable or pull the lever: 6 m crush, one use each.\n- **Over the side.** Vault the rail anywhere. Running to a cottage for ammo with the crowd behind you is the scene's best\n  moment. Oil drums on the slipway burn.\n- **Flow.** Arrival: Quill walks in during the sermon, Parishioners kneeling round the fire with their backs to him,\n  Goole on the Tannoy. Then they turn. 14 on the field at most, spawning from six alleys out of sight. Netwives from\n  minute 1. The Gutter at minute 2, heralded by his saw engine from far off. **No visible timer.** The horn sounds at\n  3:00 or at 40 kills, whichever is first. Killing the Gutter is optional and pays well.\n\n### 4.2 The Hoist\n\nThe gantry over the Line Floor. Wren cranks a fish hoist for 60 s (a progress bar on her). Foes come up two ladders and over\na conveyor. Anyone who reaches her stops the crank until you hit them. Rail hooks can be shot loose to swing through a\nlane. Wren calls flanks with odds: \"Left ladder. Eighty percent.\"\n\n### 4.3 The Funicular\n\nA fish-hoist car on a cliff cable, 90 s from foot to top, with a `driveMover` kinematic platform carrying riders. You stand\non the deck and aim freely as Parishioners leap from gantries and Netwives throw from ledges; Wren works the brake. At the\ntop the cable snaps: a large jump prompt, then a scramble up a ladder with the car falling away behind you. Failing the\nprompt costs a checkpoint, not much else.\n\n### 4.4 Small things\n\n- **Claims Practice** (Shingle): a coin-op gallery on a rotten pier, the Salvor's pride. 60 s, cardboard claimants on tracks\n  (`defineThing`, `spawnThing`), hit the frauds, spare the widows. Score pays shillings, and beating it gives a free\n  Firepower level. Enter and leave through a prompt, in the same world.\n- **Glass floats:** 12 blue glass net floats hung about the island. They shatter when shot (`addBreakables`). All 12: the Salvor\n  gives you a rare gem worth 5,000. Progress shows in the Claim File.\n- **The ledger dials** (Vault): three big number wheels, one on screen at a time. The numbers are in the 3 required ledger pages,\n  which Wren reads aloud on request. Left and right pick a wheel, up and down turn it, mouse clicks and wheel too.\n- **The seal:** a grey blob-rig seal in a net on the Shingle. Shoot the net rope to free it, and it barks and flops off. Payoff in §7.3.\n\n## 5. Player verbs and controls\n\nQuill walks and runs, never jumps. Vaults, ladders, kicks and doors are context actions on one button.\n\n| Action | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Engine action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | Left stick | W A S D | `forward back left right` |\n| Look | Right stick | Mouse (pointer lock) | |\n| Aim, hold (feet plant) | L2 (LT) | Right mouse | `aim` |\n| Fire, only while aiming | R2 (RT) | Left mouse | `fire` |\n| Use, kick, vault, climb, open | Cross (A) | E | `interact` |\n| Letter opener: slash, or parry if timed | R1 (RB) | F | `melee` |\n| Run, hold | Circle (B) | Left Shift | `sprint` |\n| Reload | Square (X) | R | `reload` |\n| Case | Triangle (Y) | Tab | `case` (view action) |\n| Wren: tap stay or follow, hold to hide | L1 (LB) | G | `partner` |\n| Quick turn 180° | R3 | C | `quickturn` |\n| Swap shoulder | L3 | V | `shoulder` (view action) |\n| Next and previous weapon | D-pad right and left | Wheel, or 1 to 5 directly | `weaponNext weaponPrev weapon1…5` |\n| Ready a throwable (aim shows the arc, fire throws) | D-pad up | Q | `throwable` |\n| Drink the best heal | D-pad down | H | `heal` |\n| Claim File (map, objectives) | Create (View) | M | `map` (view action) |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc | system `pause` |\n\nEngine codes: Cross `PadSouth`, Circle `PadEast`, Square `PadWest`, Triangle `PadNorth`, L1 `PadLeftShoulder`, R1 `PadRightShoulder`, L2\n`PadLeftTrigger`, R2 `PadRightTrigger`, L3 `PadLeftStick`, R3 `PadRightStick`, D-pad `PadUp PadDown PadLeft PadRight`, Create `PadSelect`,\nOptions `PadStart` (the system `pause`), sticks `PadLeftStickUp` and so on, wheel `WheelUp` `WheelDown`, mouse `MouseLeft` `MouseRight`.\n\nWith keyboard and mouse, when not aiming, a left click on a thing whose prompt is showing uses it. Everything rebinds on\nthe Controls screen (`input.bindings.rebind`, `input.listen`, kept by `createEngine({ store })`). In a hub session the game\nignores Options while Create is held, since that chord is the hub's.\n\n**Feel targets.** Run 3.4 m/s, sprint 5.2 m/s. Aim raise 0.22 s. Planted while aiming, turning with the view. Shot response\non the same step. Quick turn in 0.25 s (feed it through `cameraInput`). Camera arm 2.8 m, pulling in to 1.5 m while aiming.\n\n**Laser sight.** The red laser is the game's cursor: there is no crosshair. A line from the muzzle to a glowing dot where\nthe shot will go, with a small wobble (≤ 0.3°, less with Handling upgrades, added to the camera's look so it affects `aim`).\n§16.4 says how to draw it.\n\n**Aim assist (pad only, Settings, on by default).** While the centre ray is on a foe, stick look slows by 45%, plus a pull of at\nmost 2°/s toward the foe's chest. Never for the mouse.\n\n## 6. Combat\n\n### 6.1 Hit zones and reactions\n\nA shot is a ray. Work out the zone from where the ray met the capsule, relative to the actor's feet, shifted by its state\n(a kneeling foe's head is lower). Scale by build for big bodies. A zone-from-height check works [verified: head, torso and\nlegs all came back correct].\n\n| Zone (height ÷ standing height) | Damage | Reaction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Head (above 0.80) | ×2.4 | **Reel**, 0.9 s: clutches its face. Kick window. |\n| Torso (0.33 to 0.80) | ×1 | Flinch, 0.25 s. A thrower's throw is cancelled. |\n| Arm (torso height, far from the axis) | ×0.6 | Drops its weapon: it picks it up (2 s) or claws. |\n| Legs (below 0.33) | ×0.7 | **Kneel**, 1.2 s. Kick window. |\n\n**Kick (Cross/E on a reeling or kneeling foe within 2.2 m).** Roundhouse, 70 damage in a 2 m radius. Everyone in range goes\ndown as a ragdoll, gets up after about 2 s. Hero is invulnerable for 0.6 s; 80 ms hit-stop and a camera shake. This is the\ngame's central pleasure: guard it and make it land hard.\n\n**Slash (R1/F).** 18 damage, 0.45 s, breaks crates, hits downed foes for ×2. **Parry:** a press in the 0.2 s before a melee\nhit lands negates it and reels the attacker. Sparks and a clang; 120 ms hit-stop.\n\n**Grabbed.** A foe can grab you (10 damage, then 4 a second): waggle or mash to break out (§6.3). Other foes get free hits.\n\n**Lodger burst.** A foe killed by a head shot may burst into a Lodger Whip. The chance is `game.burst`, set per zone: 0 in the\nvillage, 15% in the Marsh, 30% in the Cannery, 45% at the Abbey. You never know, so head shots stay a gamble.\n\n### 6.2 Weapons\n\nFired by a **game-owned `game.guns` system** on the engine's `weapons` component, not the engine's `addWeapons` (§16.3 says why).\n\n| Weapon (flavour name) | Case cells | Mag | Shots/s | Damage | Reload | Notes | Price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Company Issue pistol, \"The Deductible\" | 3×2 | 10 | 3.2 | 28 | 1.4 s | Start weapon | free |\n| Pump gun, \"Full Coverage\" | 6×2 | 6 | 1.0 | 8 × 11 | 0.55 s a shell | 5° cone, pushes groups back | 1,800 |\n| Bolt rifle, \"The Long Policy\" | 8×2 | 5 | 0.9 | 85 | 2.6 s | Scope (hold aim + D-pad up to zoom), pierces 2 | 3,500 |\n| Trench gun, \"Premium Rate\" | 4×2 | 30 | 11 | 12 | 2.2 s | Automatic, climbing recoil | 6,000 |\n| Flare pistol | 3×2 | 1 | 1.0 | 15 + burn 40 over 4 s | 1.6 s | Kills a Lodger Whip outright, lights oil | found |\n| Letter opener | n/a | | | 18 | | Always on hand | |\n| Whaling Gun | story | 1 | | 5,000 | | Finale only (§7.3) | 1 shilling |\n\nThrowables, each with an arc preview: **Oil Bomb** (1×2, a fire pool for 5 s), **Magnesium Flash** (1×1, stuns within 8 m for\n3 s, kills exposed Lodgers), **Limpet Charge** (1×2, frag). Foes' thrown hatchets can be shot out of the air.\n\nDraw guns bigger than life (about 1.3×) so they read at 10 feet.\n\n### 6.3 Prompts under pressure\n\nOne small `qte` system: a prompt has a glyph, a window, and a result on success and failure. Three types: **press** (one button\nin a window), **mash** (fill a meter), **parry** (any `melee` in a window). Drawn huge at screen centre with a shrinking\nring. Used for the funicular jump, breaking grabs, Sedge's lunges, the finale. A dev slider scales every window.\n\n### 6.4 The Risk Rating (hidden dynamic difficulty)\n\nA hidden 0 to 10 score, `game.risk`, in the sim. Accuracy, kills without damage and speed push it up; deaths and being\nhit push it down. It scales foe aggression, attacker tokens and damage by ±15%. Settings' difficulty (Lenient, Standard,\nPunitive) sets its range and base numbers. The results screen reveals it as a joke: \"Risk rating: uninsurable\".\n\n## 7. Enemies and bosses\n\nHumanoids are **actors**, not crowd agents: 80 animated actors with clips cost about 5 ms a step [verified], and actors\nget the rich reactions, clips and ragdolls that make hits read. Hounds and Leeches use the engine's crowd rigs.\n\n### 7.1 The roster\n\n| Foe | HP | m/s | Attack | Special | First seen |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Parishioner** (oilskins, knit jumper; gaff, oar, gutting knife) | 100 | 1.25 to 1.6 | Swing 18, wind-up 0.7 s; grab | The bread and butter. Mutters scripture. | Office |\n| **Netwife** (apron, headscarf) | 80 | 1.2 | Hatchet 15, arcing at 12 m/s | Keeps 8 to 14 m. Hatchets can be shot down. | Square |\n| **The Gutter** (rubber apron, a lobster pot over his head, a motorised flensing saw on a pole) | 900 | 1.9 | Sweep 60; a grab kills below 35 HP | Engine noise from far off. Stagger only by head or knee. Pays 2,500. | Square |\n| **Bog Hound** (quadruped rig) | 60 | 5.5 | Leap bite 12 | Packs of 3 to 5. One shot staggers. | Marsh |","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":913}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FKG9b3xsNfUjHQNC9ABFPX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
